module Formatter.Logical.Comments exposing (lptAddComments)

{-| Weave parse-context comments into the Logical Printing Tree.

Comments are not part of the `Src.Module` AST. They flow through the parse
`Ctx.Context` as a separate stream of located `Line` or `Block` strings,
and have to be re-attached to the LPT before rendering — at the same
logical position the author wrote them.

# Strategy

For each comment, in source order:

1. **Find the top-level slot.** Scan the root's children — which are one
   `OriginalRows` node per top-level declaration — for the one whose row
   range covers the comment. If no declaration's range covers the row (an
   own-line comment in the gap below a declaration), splice in a fresh
   `OriginalRows` at the right sorted position — the comment detaches to
   column 1. (See "The trailing-comment boundary rule" below.)

2. **Descend to the owning subtree.** Walk down inside that slot, at each
   level picking the deepest child whose subtree's row range still
   contains the comment. Stop when no child can plausibly own the comment,
   and insert it among the current node's children just after the last
   child that ends before the comment's `(row, col)`.

3. **Extend the row range for block comments.** Multi-line block comments
   grow the enclosing `OriginalRows`' end row so the range still covers
   the whole comment, keeping later comment placements and blank-line
   insertion consistent.

# Disambiguation

Pure row-range containment is not enough — Gren source frequently has
sibling subtrees that overlap on the same row. A few tie-breakers keep
placement faithful:

- A child whose subtree starts *after* the comment is never the owner,
  even if its row range nominally contains the comment's row.
- If a later sibling's first real token is at or before the comment, the
  current child's apparent overlap is a false positive — the comment
  really sits between the two siblings. (E.g. a comment between two
  record types straddling a `->`.)
- The trailing-comment boundary rule (see below).

The insertion point also skips past synthesized (position-less) tokens at
the boundary, so a comment never lands between a real token and a
synthesized neighbour such as a generated `->` arrow.

# The trailing-comment boundary rule (idempotency)

The recurring idempotency hazard is a comment that *trails* a node's last token.
Rendered at that node's deep indent on the first format, on reparse it sits on
its own line one row down and re-attaches at the *enclosing* level — so the
indent oscillates, or drifts left to column 1 over several formats. The two
layers below cure it in different ways:

- **Top level** (`findOrCreateOrigRow`, plus `detachOwnLineTrailer`): an own-line
  comment below a top-level declaration is **never** attached to it — it always
  detaches to a fresh column-1 `OriginalRows`. This is what elm-format does, and
  column 1 is trivially a fixed point (it cannot drift). `findOrCreateOrigRow`
  answers that from the comment's source row, which misses a comment written ON
  the declaration's last row that nonetheless *renders* below it — a multi-line
  `{- … -}` with no glue row. `detachOwnLineTrailer` is the same rule asked of
  the finished tree, and it runs last for that reason. `VerticalSpace` then separates the
  detached comment from a *following* declaration with a blank line, using the
  comment's original column to tell a detached-trailing comment (indented, so it
  belonged to the construct above) from a genuine column-1 leading comment of
  what follows. (An earlier design claimed the comment as the construct's
  trailing comment and placed it by column depth — the render column was meant
  to re-trigger the claim on reparse — but the two disagreed for many shapes and
  it drifted: the claim and the placement had to agree about a column that
  the formatting itself moved.)
- **Inner descent** (`insertCommentIntoSubtree`): the formatter does not
  *descend* into a child it trails when the next sibling is a flow boundary
  (`nextSiblingIsBoundary` — the `in` keyword, the next binding, the next branch)
  or the child is one of a few box kinds that must never swallow a trailing
  comment (the box-type guards). The comment stays a sibling at the current flow
  level — between two `let` bindings, after the last binding before `in`, between
  two `when` branches, etc. The boundary test is intentionally specific, not "any
  following sibling": a container followed by a continuation (e.g. a record type
  before its `->`) also has a sibling, but a comment before that record's close
  belongs inside it.

The exception in both layers is `commentInsideTrailingBracket`: a comment still
*inside* a container before its closing bracket genuinely belongs there, so it
descends. For that to work a container with a representable close must register
it via `lpnBracketNode` (see "adding a construct" below).

# Adding support for a new construct

When a new Gren construct gains an LPT shape, comment handling usually needs
*no* change here: emit its pieces as ordinary boxes in a flow and the
trailing-comment rule above places boundary comments correctly. Two things to
get right so it stays idempotent:

1. If the construct has a closing bracket / delimiter the parser discards, build
   its box with `lpnBracketNode <closePos>` so `commentInsideTrailingBracket`
   can tell "inside the brackets" from "past them". This applies to a plain
   `( … )` as much as to a record or an array: an author-written PAREN around a
   TYPE went years without it, and a comment before its `)` escaped the parens
   and then landed on the other side of the `->`'s zero-width anchor on reparse
   -- a genuine non-idempotency. See `fixupParensChildrenNodesAt`.
2. Run `effectful-tests/fuzz-idempotency.py` (it is *not* part of `run-tests.sh`).
   If it flags a trailing-comment gap, the fix belongs in one of the two places
   above — not in a new construct-specific branch.

# Indent redirection

When the insertion lands immediately before an `IndentedBlock`, a
`PipelineStep`, or a `BodyBlock`, the comment is pushed *inside* that wrapper as
its first child rather than placed as a preceding sibling. All three start their
content on a fresh line when rendered, so this nesting makes a comment at the
start of a body render at the body's own column rather than gluing onto the head
that precedes it (`x =` ⏎ `{- c -}` ⏎ `42`, not `x = {- c -}`).
-}

import Array.Builder as Builder

import Compiler.Parse.Context as Ctx
import Compiler.SourcePosition exposing (Located, Position, at)

import Formatter.Logical.LogicalPrintingTree exposing
    ( CommentRole(..)
    , LPBox(..)
    , LPNode
    , ListBrackets(..)
    , RecordKind(..)
    , SyntaxType(..)
    , commentLeadsNext
    , commentRendersOwnLine
    , commentRole
    , firstRowInSubtree
    , gluesTrailingComment
    , lastRowInSubtree
    , lpnBox
    , lpnBracketEndElastic
    , lpnBracketEndExact
    , lpnBracketStart
    , lpnChildren
    , lpnExtendElasticBracket
    , lpnFirstPos
    , lpnLastBracketEnd
    , lpnLastPos
    , lpnLeaf
    , lpnNode
    , lpnReplaceChildren
    , lpnSetCommentRole
    , spanTrailingOwnLine
    , subtreeRowRange
    )
import Formatter.Strings exposing (countNewlines)


{-| Threaded across the whole per-comment fold in `lptAddComments`. Comments
arrive in source (row) order, and the root's top-level children are already
in sorted, non-overlapping row order (`MakeLogical.sortOriginalRows`), so once
a comment's row moves past a child's range, no *later* comment can ever match
that child again — it can be permanently retired into `settled`. `target` is
the one child that might still be receiving comments (kept out of `settled`
so a re-hit doesn't require re-finding it); `rest` is everything strictly
after `target`, untouched.

This exists because the naive per-comment approach — re-scan ALL of the
root's children from the start, every single comment, then rebuild the whole
children array — is O(n) per call (`findOrCreateOrigRow`'s scan, and the
`before ++ [x] ++ after` rebuild) driven by an O(n) outer loop (one call per
comment): O(n^2) on a file with many top-level comments (`wide-comments-only`
in `pathological-other.py`). Settling `before` into `settled` (an
`Array.Builder`, amortized O(1) per element) the moment it's known to be
unreachable, and leaving `target`/`rest` untouched on a re-hit (the common
case — several comments on the same declaration), makes the total work
across the whole comment sequence O(n + m) amortized: each child is scanned
past / settled at most once, and `rest` is only ever resliced when the
search actually needs to move past `target`. -}
type alias CommentState =
    { settled : Builder.Builder LPNode
    , target : Maybe LPNode
    , rest : Array LPNode
    }


lptAddComments : Ctx.Context -> LPNode -> LPNode
lptAddComments ctx root =
    let
        initial =
            { settled = Builder.empty (Array.length (lpnChildren root))
            , target = Nothing
            , rest = lpnChildren root
            }

        final =
            Array.foldl addOneComment initial (Builder.toArray ctx.comments)

        finalChildren =
            (when final.target is
                Just t ->
                    Builder.pushLast t final.settled

                Nothing ->
                    final.settled
            )
                |> Builder.append final.rest
                |> Builder.toArray
    in
    lpnReplaceChildren root
        (Array.mapAndFlatten detachOwnLineTrailer finalChildren
            |> Array.map rehomePipelineStepTrailers
        )


{-| Move each pipeline step's trailing OWN-LINE comment run onto the front of
the step that follows it.

Such a run renders below its step, which puts it above the next `|>` — and a
comment on a row of its own before an operator is, to a reparse, that operator's
step's LEADING comment. There is no other reading available. So the first format
has to own it there too, or the two formats disagree about something neither of
them renders differently.

`Render/MakeRenderBox`'s step renderer already made the two owners produce the
same BYTES for the run itself (`ea1c2ab`). That was not enough: the run's owner
also decides `glueLeading`, which asks `commentTextCanRide` of the whole leading
run all-or-nothing. With the run split across two steps the first format saw
only the ridable `{- c -}` and glued it onto the `|>`'s row; the reparse, seeing
both comments in one run, refused — because one of them is a multi-line
`{- … -}` that cannot ride — and stacked it instead. Same comment, same role,
same rendering rule, different answer, for ever
(`KitchenComments.formatted.gren[multi]@2121`).

The LAST step keeps its own trailing run: there is no following step to give it
to, and what happens to it there is `detachOwnLineTrailer`'s question, already
answered above.

Applied after `detachOwnLineTrailer` so a run that leaves the declaration
entirely is already gone, and recursively, since a pipeline can sit anywhere. -}
rehomePipelineStepTrailers : LPNode -> LPNode
rehomePipelineStepTrailers node =
    let
        withRebuiltChildren =
            lpnReplaceChildren node
                (Array.map rehomePipelineStepTrailers (lpnChildren node))
    in
    when lpnBox withRebuiltChildren is
        Pipeline _ ->
            lpnReplaceChildren withRebuiltChildren
                (movePipelineStepTrailers (lpnChildren withRebuiltChildren))

        _ ->
            withRebuiltChildren


{-| The left-to-right sweep `rehomePipelineStepTrailers` runs over one
pipeline's children: each step takes the run the previous step handed it, then
hands its own trailing own-line run to the next. A run left over at the end
belongs to the last step, which keeps it. -}
movePipelineStepTrailers : Array LPNode -> Array LPNode
movePipelineStepTrailers children =
    let
        step acc child =
            when lpnBox child is
                PipelineStep ->
                    let
                        kids =
                            acc.pending ++ lpnChildren child

                        split =
                            spanTrailingOwnLine (\n -> n) kids
                    in
                    { pending = split.trailing
                    , out = Array.pushLast (lpnReplaceChildren child split.body) acc.out
                    , lastStep = Just (Array.length acc.out)
                    , lastKids = kids
                    }

                _ ->
                    { acc | out = Array.pushLast child acc.out }

        final =
            Array.foldl (\child acc -> step acc child)
                { pending = [], out = [], lastStep = Nothing, lastKids = [] }
                children
    in
    if Array.isEmpty final.pending then
        final.out

    else
        -- Nothing followed the last step, so it keeps the run it was going to
        -- hand on — and keeps it EXACTLY WHERE IT WAS, which is what `lastKids`
        -- (this step's children before its own peel) restores.
        --
        -- Appending the run to the step's children instead is not the same
        -- thing, and the difference is a real bug: the peel descends no further
        -- than the step's own child list, but a trailing run can be nested
        -- deeper — inside the operand flow — and re-appending LIFTS it out of
        -- there. That changes what `detachOwnLineTrailer` then sees, and a run
        -- past a declaration's last token stopped being detached to column 1,
        -- where the reparse puts it (`KitchenComments`, a `{-c99-}` after the
        -- final `|>` step). Nothing is handed on, so nothing should move.
        when final.lastStep is
            Just i ->
                Array.indexedMap
                    (\j n ->
                        if j == i then
                            lpnReplaceChildren n final.lastKids

                        else
                            n
                    )
                    final.out

            Nothing ->
                -- No step at all took the pending run, so there is no step in
                -- this pipeline; put it back where it was rather than drop it.
                final.out ++ final.pending


{-| The top-level trailing-comment boundary rule, asked of the TREE instead of
of the source rows.

`findOrCreateOrigRow` already refuses to attach a comment written on a row
*below* a declaration: it detaches to a fresh column-1 `OriginalRows`, which is
what elm-format does and is trivially a fixed point. A comment written on the
declaration's LAST row escapes that check — its row is inside the declaration's
range — and yet it can still end up rendering below the declaration. A
multi-line `{- … -}` that finds no glue row classifies `LeadsOwnLine`, which
renders on a fresh line under the last token:

    v =                       first format:        and then, for ever:
        fn a b {- c           v =                  v =
       second -}                 fn a b                fn a b
                                 {- c             {- c
                                    second -}        second -}

The reparse then sees exactly the shape `findOrCreateOrigRow` refuses, detaches
it to column 1, and the two formats disagree. Same class as `45f7269`: a
placement decided from a source row that this pass itself is about to invalidate.

So once every comment is placed, lift any trailing comment run that renders
own-line into the same column-1 `OriginalRows` the reparse would give it — the
first format builds the tree the second one would.

**Scoped to the part of the run that renders own-line** — `peelOwnLineTrailingRun`
hands back exactly that suffix, and the members before it stay on the
declaration's last row. A trailing comment that GLUES (`TrailsPrevious` / `RidesInline`)
stays on the declaration's last row, is already a fixed point, and belongs to
the code it was written next to (C1) — `[ 1` ⏎ `, 2` ⏎ `] {- c` ⏎ `second -}`
keeps its comment on the `]` row. elm-format detaches that one too, and the
broader rule "any multi-line comment past the last token detaches" would gain
that parity; it is deliberately not taken here, because it would change shapes
that are not broken. Only the ones that render below the declaration are.

The whole run moves together (C1 run cohesion) and only the first member becomes
`Standalone`; a `-- c` glued behind it keeps `TrailsPrevious`, which is the tree
the reparse produces.
-}
detachOwnLineTrailer : LPNode -> Array LPNode
detachOwnLineTrailer child =
    when lpnBox child is
        OriginalRows rowInfo ->
            if hostsOwnLineTrailer rowInfo.stype then
                let
                    peeled =
                        peelOwnLineTrailingRun child
                in
                when Array.popFirst peeled.run is
                    Just { first = leadComment, rest = restOfRun } ->
                        let
                            runNodes =
                                Array.pushFirst (lpnSetCommentRole Standalone leadComment) restOfRun

                            runFirstRow =
                                firstRowInSubtree leadComment

                            runLastRow =
                                Array.foldl (\n acc -> max acc (lastRowInSubtree n)) runFirstRow runNodes

                            trimmedDecl =
                                lpnNode
                                    (OriginalRows { rowInfo | last = lastRowInSubtree peeled.node })
                                    (lpnChildren peeled.node)
                        in
                        [ trimmedDecl
                        , lpnNode
                            (OriginalRows
                                { first = runFirstRow
                                , last = runLastRow
                                , stype = commentRunStype leadComment
                                }
                            )
                            runNodes
                        ]

                    Nothing ->
                        [ child ]

            else
                [ child ]

        _ ->
            [ child ]


{-| The suffix of the node's trailing comment run that renders *below* the
declaration, removed from the node.

Descends through the last non-comment child, because that is where the previous
level's run continues: the order on the page is *code, inner run, outer
trailers*, so the inner run is appended before this level's own trailing
comments.

**The suffix starts at the earliest member that renders below**, and the members
before it stay where they are. That split is why the boundary rule lives here
rather than in the caller: a run's leader can perfectly well glue onto the
declaration's last row while a later member cannot, and only the peel knows
where in the tree each member sits — putting a kept prefix back afterwards would
have to re-nest comments the descent had already lifted out.

Two things make a member render below, and `tailComment` is what carries the
second across a level boundary:

  - `runRendersBelowDeclaration` — the member brings its own rows.
  - **the member before it is a `--`**, which swallows the rest of its row, so
    everything after it is on a later one whatever its own kind says. That is
    the effect-module header's shape: a `--` inside `where { … }` escapes to the
    header's tail (see `docs/knownLimitations.md`), and the `{- note -}` the
    author wrote after `exposing (..)` — on a row the header still covers, so it
    is attached here — is pushed onto a fresh row below it. The reparse, seeing
    it past the header's last row, gives it a column-1 `OriginalRows`; this is
    the pass that builds that tree first.

A container whose children are **all** comments is left alone (`front` empty),
rather than emptied — an empty `BodyBlock` is not a declaration body, and no
shape that reaches here needs it. -}
peelOwnLineTrailingRun : LPNode -> { node : LPNode, run : Array LPNode, tailComment : Maybe LPNode }
peelOwnLineTrailingRun node =
    let
        children =
            lpnChildren node

        split =
            Array.foldr
                (\c acc ->
                    if acc.stillTrailing && isCommentNode c then
                        { acc | trailing = Array.pushFirst c acc.trailing }

                    else
                        { stillTrailing = False, front = Array.pushFirst c acc.front, trailing = acc.trailing }
                )
                { stillTrailing = True, front = [], trailing = [] }
                children
    in
    when Array.popLast split.front is
        Nothing ->
            { node = node, run = [], tailComment = Nothing }

        Just { last = lastCode, initial = front } ->
            let
                inner =
                    if descendsForTrailingRun (lpnBox lastCode) then
                        peelOwnLineTrailingRun lastCode

                    else
                        { node = lastCode, run = [], tailComment = Nothing }

                cut =
                    if Array.isEmpty inner.run then
                        firstRowOfItsOwn inner.tailComment split.trailing

                    else
                        -- The run is already open from a deeper level: every
                        -- comment after it is below the declaration too.
                        0

                run =
                    inner.run ++ Array.dropFirst cut split.trailing
            in
            if Array.isEmpty run then
                { node = node
                , run = []
                , tailComment =
                    when Array.last split.trailing is
                        Just c ->
                            Just c

                        Nothing ->
                            inner.tailComment
                }

            else
                { node =
                    lpnReplaceChildren node
                        (Array.pushLast inner.node front ++ Array.takeFirst cut split.trailing)
                , run = run
                , tailComment = Nothing
                }


{-| The index in a trailing comment run at which the members stop sharing the
declaration's last row. `before` is the comment that precedes the run on the
page, if the node's own tail already ended in one.

Returns the run's length when every member stays put, which is the "nothing to
detach" answer. -}
firstRowOfItsOwn : Maybe LPNode -> Array LPNode -> Int
firstRowOfItsOwn before items =
    let
        scan =
            Array.foldl
                (\c acc ->
                    if acc.found then
                        acc

                    else if runRendersBelowDeclaration c || endsItsRow acc.prev then
                        { acc | found = True }

                    else
                        { index = acc.index + 1, prev = Just c, found = False }
                )
                { index = 0, prev = before, found = False }
                items
    in
    scan.index


{-| Whether this comment, if there is one, takes the rest of its row with it —
true of a `--` and of nothing else. A `{- … -}` of any shape can be followed on
its closing row. -}
endsItsRow : Maybe LPNode -> Bool
endsItsRow prev =
    when prev is
        Just node ->
            when lpnBox node is
                SingleLineComment _ ->
                    True

                _ ->
                    False

        Nothing ->
            False


{-| Whether this run member is one that renders on a fresh row *below* the
declaration's last token by its own shape — the first of the two things the
peel splits on (a `--` in front of it is the other).

Two conditions, and both are needed:

  - `LeadsOwnLine`. A run that glues (`TrailsPrevious` / `RidesInline`) stays on
    the declaration's last row, is already a fixed point, and belongs to the
    code it was written next to.
  - **the comment brings its own rows** — a `{- … -}` written across more than
    one row. `LeadsOwnLine` alone is not "renders own-line": the render layer
    keeps a *single-line* block comment inline regardless of the role, which is
    why `[ 1 ] {- one -} {- two -}` stays on its row, and why lifting on the
    role alone broke nine fixtures including that one.

A `--` is deliberately **not** included even though it also ends its row.
Several fixtures pin an own-line `--` staying at the construct's indent below a
wrapped import and below a pipeline's last step; whether those shapes should
detach is a separate question with its own fixtures, not a free rider on this
one. -}
runRendersBelowDeclaration : LPNode -> Bool
runRendersBelowDeclaration node =
    -- The one spelling of "does this comment render on a line of its own",
    -- which `spanTrailingOwnLine`'s run extension needs too and therefore lives
    -- beside `CommentRole` (same reason as `roleGlues`). This function is the
    -- boundary rule; the predicate under it is not its own.
    commentRendersOwnLine node


{-| Which `OriginalRows` kinds `detachOwnLineTrailer` applies to.

`isDeclStype` is the wrong question here, and deliberately so: it also decides
which nodes a *leading* comment may glue onto and which count as covering a row
(`coveredByOtherDecl`), and the module header answers those two differently — it
has no keyword column a leading comment can pair with, and it is not a
declaration a later comment can trail into.

But for a comment written past the header's last token it behaves exactly like
any other top-level node: an effect module's `where { … }` block makes the
header multi-line, so a trailing multi-line `{- … -}` finds no glue row,
classifies `LeadsOwnLine`, and renders on a fresh row *underneath* the header —
which the reparse then re-homes to a column-1 `Standalone`, the same
disagreement `detachOwnLineTrailer` exists to remove. elm-format detaches this
one too (it detaches a trailing block comment after a *one-row* header as well,
where gren still glues — a separate, stable divergence this does not touch).
-}
hostsOwnLineTrailer : SyntaxType -> Bool
hostsOwnLineTrailer st =
    when st is
        StModule ->
            True

        _ ->
            isDeclStype st


{-| Whether the trailing-run peel may look inside this container for the run's
earlier members.

Only a **flow wrapper** — one that renders its children and nothing after them.
A container that renders a closing delimiter (`}` `]` `)`) does not qualify: the
delimiter is not a node, so its last child having no node after it does not mean
it has no *token* after it. `{ rec | fld = x -- c` ⏎ `}` is the shape — the `--`
is the record update's last child and the `}` still follows it, so it is not a
declaration trailer and must not be lifted out. (This is the same stand-in-token
trap `commentSplitsType` was deleted over (see `docs/llmAttic.md`), met from the
other side; ignoring it here made the fix a no-op on its own reproducer, since
the `--` joined the run and its `TrailsPrevious` role vetoed the lift.)

Anything not listed stops the descent. That is the safe default: a missed lift
leaves an existing non-idempotency in place, where a wrong one would move a
comment that has a rendered token after it.

`WhenFlow` / `WhenBranch` qualify on exactly that test — a `when` has no closing
delimiter, and its last branch's body is the last thing it renders. A comment
past that body was rendering at the branch's indent while the reparse, seeing it
past the declaration's last token, re-homed it to column 1. Only the LAST branch
is reachable here, since the peel descends through the last child alone; a
comment trailing an earlier branch has a following sibling and stays where it
is. -}
descendsForTrailingRun : LPBox -> Bool
descendsForTrailingRun box =
    when box is
        BodyBlock ->
            True

        IndentedBlock ->
            True

        SoftIndentedBlock ->
            True

        AcrossOrVertical _ ->
            True

        Binop _ ->
            True

        OpAndRhs ->
            True

        Pipeline _ ->
            True

        PipelineStep ->
            True

        WhenFlow _ ->
            True

        WhenBranch ->
            True

        _ ->
            False


commentRunStype : LPNode -> SyntaxType
commentRunStype node =
    when lpnBox node is
        SingleLineComment _ ->
            StSingleLineComment

        _ ->
            StBlockComment


addOneComment : Located Ctx.Comment -> CommentState -> CommentState
addOneComment locComment state =
    when locComment.value is
        Ctx.Line _ ->
            -- `tryLeadingGluedAttach` only ever matches a block comment (see
            -- its own `Ctx.Line _ -> Nothing` case) — skip straight to the
            -- generic path rather than paying for the check.
            addCommentGeneric locComment state

        Ctx.Block _ ->
            when tryLeadingGluedAttach locComment state is
                Just gluedState ->
                    gluedState

                Nothing ->
                    addCommentGeneric locComment state


{-| The shared tail of comment placement, used both when a comment re-hits
the current `target` (no search needed) and when `advanceAndSearch` has just
found or created the target via `findOrCreateOrigRow`. Exactly the former
body of `lptAddCommentGeneric` from `commentNode` onward, factored out so
both call sites share it verbatim. -}
applyCommentToOrigRow : Located Ctx.Comment -> LPNode -> Bool -> LPNode -> LPNode
applyCommentToOrigRow locComment baseCommentNode created origRowNode =
    let
        row =
            locComment.start.row

        col =
            locComment.start.col

        -- A comment that detaches to a fresh column-1 `OriginalRows` (see
        -- `findOrCreateOrigRow`) is `Standalone` — the empty-container branch of
        -- `insertAmongChildren` keeps this role. Otherwise the role is decided at
        -- the splice point by `classifyCommentKind`.
        commentNode =
            if created then
                lpnSetCommentRole Standalone baseCommentNode

            else
                baseCommentNode

        updatedOrigRow =
            insertCommentIntoSubtree commentNode row col origRowNode

        endRow =
            when locComment.value is
                Ctx.Line _ ->
                    row

                Ctx.Block content ->
                    row + countNewlines content

        -- A comment pinned inside an elastic container (the module header's
        -- `exposing ( … )`) pushes that container's `)` below itself, and the
        -- declaration's range has to reach the `)` too — otherwise the next
        -- comment of a trailing chain lands past the declaration and detaches
        -- to column 1 instead of joining the chain above the `)`.
        elasticEnd =
            if lpnBracketEndElastic updatedOrigRow then
                when lpnLastBracketEnd updatedOrigRow is
                    Just closeEnd ->
                        closeEnd.row

                    Nothing ->
                        -1

            else
                -1

        coveredRow =
            max endRow elasticEnd
    in
    when lpnBox updatedOrigRow is
        OriginalRows rowInfo ->
            let
                -- The header's other derived close moves for the same reason,
                -- and is recomputed rather than grown because it is derived from
                -- the block's contents, which this comment has just changed.
                covered =
                    max coveredRow (moduleWhereCloseRow rowInfo.stype updatedOrigRow)
            in
            if covered > rowInfo.last then
                lpnNode
                    (OriginalRows { rowInfo | last = covered })
                    (lpnChildren updatedOrigRow)

            else
                updatedOrigRow

        _ ->
            updatedOrigRow


{-| The row an effect module's wrapped `where { … }` block renders its `}` on:
one below everything the block now holds, or `-1` when there is no such block or
it still fits on one row.

Both that `}` and the `exposing` after it are position-less, so nothing in the
header records this row. `MakeLogical.moduleLineClosesBelow` bumps the header's
`OriginalRows` range by it at build time — from the FIELD rows alone, because
that is all there is before comments are placed. A comment landing inside the
block pushes the block's contents down and the `}` with them, so the bump has to
move too; otherwise a comment the author wrote on the `}` row falls past the
header's range, detaches to column 1, and disagrees with the format that
produced that row and glued it there.

Exactly the job `elasticEnd` does for the `exposing ( … )` list's derived `)`,
asked of the header's one other derived close. It is *recomputed* rather than
grown, because it is derived from the block's contents — which the comment just
being placed may have changed. The block is deliberately **not** an
`lpnElasticBracketNode`: that says "anything reaching this container is inside
it", which is true of the exposing list (nothing follows it) and false here —
`exposing (..)` does.
-}
moduleWhereCloseRow : SyntaxType -> LPNode -> Int
moduleWhereCloseRow stype node =
    if stype /= StModule then
        -1

    else
        Array.foldl
            (\child acc ->
                when lpnBox child is
                    AllAcrossOrAllVertical (ListCurly _) ->
                        let
                            lastRow =
                                lastRowInSubtree child
                        in
                        if lastRow > firstRowInSubtree child then
                            max acc (lastRow + 1)

                        else
                            acc

                    _ ->
                        acc
            )
            -1
            (lpnChildren node)


addCommentGeneric : Located Ctx.Comment -> CommentState -> CommentState
addCommentGeneric locComment state =
    let
        baseCommentNode =
            lpnLeaf (mkCommentLpBox locComment)

        row =
            locComment.start.row

        stype =
            when locComment.value is
                Ctx.Line _ ->
                    StSingleLineComment

                Ctx.Block _ ->
                    StBlockComment
    in
    when state.target is
        Just t ->
            when lpnBox t is
                OriginalRows { first, last } ->
                    if first <= row && row <= last then
                        -- Re-hit: the comment still belongs to `target`.
                        -- `rest` is untouched — no search, no rebuild.
                        { state | target = Just (applyCommentToOrigRow locComment baseCommentNode False t) }

                    else
                        advanceAndSearch locComment baseCommentNode row stype { state | settled = Builder.pushLast t state.settled, target = Nothing }

                _ ->
                    advanceAndSearch locComment baseCommentNode row stype { state | settled = Builder.pushLast t state.settled, target = Nothing }

        Nothing ->
            advanceAndSearch locComment baseCommentNode row stype state


{-| `state.target` doesn't own this comment (or there is none yet): scan
`state.rest` — unchanged from `findOrCreateOrigRow`'s existing search+create
logic — to find or create the new target, permanently settling everything
found strictly before it. -}
advanceAndSearch : Located Ctx.Comment -> LPNode -> Int -> SyntaxType -> CommentState -> CommentState
advanceAndSearch locComment baseCommentNode row stype state =
    let
        { origRowNode, before, after, created } =
            findOrCreateOrigRow row stype state.rest

        newTarget =
            applyCommentToOrigRow locComment baseCommentNode created origRowNode
    in
    { settled = Builder.append before state.settled
    , target = Just newTarget
    , rest = after
    }


mkCommentLpBox : Located Ctx.Comment -> LPBox
mkCommentLpBox locComment =
    let
        start =
            locComment.start

        end =
            locComment.end
    in
    -- The role is a placeholder here; `classifyCommentRole` assigns the real
    -- one at the splice point, where the neighbouring siblings are in hand.
    when locComment.value is
        Ctx.Line string ->
            SingleLineComment { loc = at start end string, role = LeadsOwnLine }

        Ctx.Block string ->
            BlockComment { loc = at start end string, role = LeadsOwnLine }


-- LEADING GLUED BLOCK COMMENT
--
-- A block comment the author wrote glued to the *front* of a top-level
-- declaration's line — its closing delimiter ends on the declaration's
-- first-token row, before that token — stays glued on that line (single-line
-- `{- c -} import Qux`, or multi-line whose final row carries the keyword). It
-- attaches as a leading child of that declaration's `OriginalRows`, role
-- `LeadsInline`, so it travels with the declaration when an import run sorts and
-- renders glued (see `CommentRole`). This is the front-of-line mirror of a
-- `TrailsPrevious` trailing comment, and — unlike the generic path, which keys
-- off the comment's *start* row — it keys off the comment's *end* row so the
-- multi-line shape is recognised too.


{-| If `locComment` is a block comment glued to the front of a top-level
declaration, attach it there and return the updated `CommentState`; otherwise
`Nothing` (the generic placement runs).

Searches only `state.target` (if any) plus `state.rest` — never
`state.settled` — because a comment can only glue to a declaration whose
range has not yet been passed: `state.settled` holds only children strictly
before every remaining (and hence this) comment's row (see `CommentState`'s
doc comment), and `coveredByOtherDecl`'s `first <= row && row <= last` check
can never hold for one of them. Searching the smaller active window instead
of the whole tree is what keeps this from being an O(n) rescan on every block
comment. -}
tryLeadingGluedAttach : Located Ctx.Comment -> CommentState -> Maybe CommentState
tryLeadingGluedAttach locComment state =
    when locComment.value is
        Ctx.Line _ ->
            Nothing

        Ctx.Block _ ->
            let
                startPos =
                    locComment.start

                endPos =
                    locComment.end

                activeChildren =
                    when state.target is
                        Just t ->
                            [ t ] ++ state.rest

                        Nothing ->
                            state.rest

                -- The declaration this comment is glued to: a declaration
                -- `OriginalRows` whose first row is the comment's end row, where
                -- the comment leads the declaration's content (see
                -- `commentLeadsDecl` — the rule differs for imports, whose keyword
                -- carries no real column), and whose gluing does not steal a
                -- comment that trails a previous declaration (`coveredByOtherDecl`).
                target =
                    findIndexed
                        (\i child ->
                            when lpnBox child is
                                OriginalRows { stype } ->
                                    isDeclStype stype
                                        -- The comment's closing delimiter ends on
                                        -- the declaration's first-token row. Using
                                        -- the token row (not the OriginalRows
                                        -- `first`) is what lets a *second* leading
                                        -- comment match after the first already
                                        -- extended `first` up to its own row.
                                        && (when firstTokenPos child is
                                                Just tokPos ->
                                                    tokPos.row == endPos.row

                                                Nothing ->
                                                    False
                                           )
                                        && commentLeadsDecl stype child startPos
                                        && not (coveredByOtherDecl i startPos.row activeChildren)

                                _ ->
                                    False
                        )
                        activeChildren
            in
            when target is
                Nothing ->
                    Nothing

                Just { index } ->
                    let
                        commentNode =
                            lpnSetCommentRole LeadsInline (lpnLeaf (mkCommentLpBox locComment))

                        updatedChildren =
                            Array.indexedMap
                                (\i child ->
                                    if i == index then
                                        attachLeadingChild startPos commentNode child

                                    else
                                        child
                                )
                                activeChildren
                    in
                    Just (restoreState state updatedChildren)


{-| Rebuild `state` from `activeChildren` — the same `[target?] ++ rest`
sequence `tryLeadingGluedAttach` searched, with exactly one element possibly
replaced — splitting it back into `target`/`rest` at the same boundary. -}
restoreState : CommentState -> Array LPNode -> CommentState
restoreState state activeChildren =
    when state.target is
        Just _ ->
            when Array.popFirst activeChildren is
                Just { first = newTarget, rest = newRest } ->
                    { state | target = Just newTarget, rest = newRest }

                Nothing ->
                    state

        Nothing ->
            { state | rest = activeChildren }


{-| Insert `commentNode` among a declaration's children at the position dictated
by the comment's start, and extend the declaration's recorded `first` row up to
the comment's start row so the range stays consistent for later passes.
-}
attachLeadingChild : Position -> LPNode -> LPNode -> LPNode
attachLeadingChild startPos commentNode declNode =
    let
        children =
            lpnChildren declNode

        -- Leading comments precede the declaration's first real token. Walk the
        -- existing leading-comment prefix (comment children that start before
        -- this one) into `head`, and stop at the first non-comment child — the
        -- keyword — inserting the new comment there. Stopping at the keyword
        -- (rather than comparing columns against it) is what makes a second
        -- leading comment land *before* an import's keyword, whose recorded
        -- column is pinned to 1 and so cannot be compared against.
        placement =
            Array.foldl
                (\child acc ->
                    if acc.placed then
                        { acc | rest = Array.pushLast child acc.rest }

                    else if
                        isCommentNode child
                            && (when lpnFirstPos child is
                                    Just p ->
                                        posStrictlyBefore p startPos

                                    Nothing ->
                                        False
                               )
                    then
                        { acc | head = Array.pushLast child acc.head }

                    else
                        { acc | rest = Array.pushLast child acc.rest, placed = True }
                )
                { head = [], rest = [], placed = False }
                children

        newChildren =
            placement.head ++ [ commentNode ] ++ placement.rest
    in
    when lpnBox declNode is
        OriginalRows rowInfo ->
            lpnNode
                (OriginalRows { rowInfo | first = min rowInfo.first startPos.row })
                newChildren

        _ ->
            lpnNode (lpnBox declNode) newChildren


{-| True when a block comment starting at `startPos` (its end row already known
to equal the declaration's first row) *leads* the declaration `child` — sits
before the declaration's real content — rather than sitting after the keyword.

For a function / alias / union / port the first real token carries a true source
column, so "no token starts before the comment" settles it, and multiple leading
comments each pass (each starts before the name).

An `import` keyword carries **no** real column (it is pinned to column 1 in
`MakeLogical.processImport`, and the AST import region also starts at column 1),
so that test can't be used. Instead a leading comment run is recognised by being
**anchored at column 1**: the comment itself starts at column 1, or a
sibling comment already attached to this import starts at column 1 and ends on
the same row (the run's anchor) — and it sits before the module name. A comment
written *after* the keyword (`import {- c -} Zebra`) has no column-1 anchor, so
it is left to the generic path. -}
commentLeadsDecl : SyntaxType -> LPNode -> Position -> Bool
commentLeadsDecl stype child startPos =
    when stype is
        StImport ->
            (startPos.col == 1 || hasCol1CommentChild child startPos.row)
                && (when importModuleNamePos child is
                        Just namePos ->
                            posStrictlyBefore startPos namePos

                        Nothing ->
                            False
                   )

        _ ->
            when firstTokenPos child is
                Just tokPos ->
                    not (posStrictlyBefore tokPos startPos)

                Nothing ->
                    False


{-| The source position of an import's module name — the first non-comment child
after the (column-1-pinned) `import` keyword. -}
importModuleNamePos : LPNode -> Maybe Position
importModuleNamePos node =
    lpnChildren node
        |> Array.keepIf (\n -> not (isCommentNode n))
        |> Array.get 1
        |> Maybe.andThen lpnFirstPos


{-| True when this declaration already has a leading comment child that starts at
column 1 and ends on `row` — the column-1 anchor of an import's leading run, so a
second glued comment on the same row is recognised as leading too. -}
hasCol1CommentChild : LPNode -> Int -> Bool
hasCol1CommentChild node row =
    Array.any
        (\child ->
            isCommentNode child
                && (when lpnFirstPos child is
                        Just p ->
                            p.col == 1 && lastRowInSubtree child == row

                        Nothing ->
                            False
                   )
        )
        (lpnChildren node)


{-| The position of a declaration node's first non-comment token. -}
firstTokenPos : LPNode -> Maybe Position
firstTokenPos node =
    Array.foldl
        (\child acc ->
            when acc is
                Just _ ->
                    acc

                Nothing ->
                    if isCommentNode child then
                        Nothing

                    else
                        lpnFirstPos child
        )
        Nothing
        (lpnChildren node)


{-| True when some declaration `OriginalRows` other than the one at `skipIndex`
covers `row` in its `[first, last]` range — i.e. the comment starts inside a
previous declaration and should trail it, not lead this one. -}
coveredByOtherDecl : Int -> Int -> Array LPNode -> Bool
coveredByOtherDecl skipIndex row nodes =
    Array.indexedMap
        (\i node ->
            if i == skipIndex then
                False

            else
                when lpnBox node is
                    OriginalRows { first, last, stype } ->
                        isDeclStype stype && first <= row && row <= last

                    _ ->
                        False
        )
        nodes
        |> Array.any (\x -> x)


isDeclStype : SyntaxType -> Bool
isDeclStype st =
    when st is
        StImport ->
            True

        StFunctionSignature ->
            True

        StFunctionDeclaration ->
            True

        StTypeAlias ->
            True

        StTypeUnion ->
            True

        StPort ->
            True

        StInfixDecl ->
            True

        _ ->
            False


{-| `a` is strictly before `b`: an earlier row, or the same row and a strictly
earlier column. Compare `posAtOrBefore`, which is inclusive on the column and
takes its position as a loose row/col pair -- the two differ in that one `=`,
and this module is where off-by-one row logic is the whole game, so the names
carry the comparison. -}
posStrictlyBefore : Position -> Position -> Bool
posStrictlyBefore a b =
    a.row < b.row || (a.row == b.row && a.col < b.col)


findIndexed : (Int -> a -> Bool) -> Array a -> Maybe { index : Int, value : a }
findIndexed predicate arr =
    Array.foldl
        (\v acc ->
            when acc.found is
                Just _ ->
                    acc

                Nothing ->
                    if predicate acc.index v then
                        { acc | found = Just { index = acc.index, value = v } }

                    else
                        { acc | index = acc.index + 1 }
        )
        { index = 0, found = Nothing }
        arr
        |> .found


-- PHASE 1: FIND THE TOP-LEVEL SLOT
--
-- Pick the root-child OriginalRows whose row range covers the comment, or splice
-- in a fresh column-1 OriginalRows when none does. A comment in the gap below a
-- declaration always takes the fresh-row path — it detaches to column 1 (the
-- top-level half of the trailing-comment boundary rule).


{-
   Scans root's children for an OriginalRows whose range includes row.
   Returns { origRowNode, before, after } where before ++ [origRowNode] ++ after
   reconstructs the children array with that node at its original position.
   If no match is found, creates a new OriginalRows node inserted in sorted position.
-}
findOrCreateOrigRow :
    Int
    -> SyntaxType
    -> Array LPNode
    ->
        { origRowNode : LPNode
        , before : Array LPNode
        , after : Array LPNode
        , created : Bool
        }
findOrCreateOrigRow row stype nodes =
    let
        -- This runs once per top-level comment (called from
        -- `lptAddCommentGeneric`'s per-comment loop), each time scanning the
        -- WHOLE root-children array built so far — already O(n) per call. Both
        -- folds below used to additionally build `before`/`after` with
        -- `Array.pushLast`, which is itself O(n) per call (`Array.toSpliced`,
        -- a full-array copy) — one O(n) rebuild PER NODE VISITED inside an
        -- already O(n)-per-call scan, i.e. O(n^2) work inside a routine that's
        -- itself called O(n) times: cubic on a file that's mostly top-level
        -- comments. `Array.Builder` makes each fold's own accumulation O(n),
        -- leaving the overall cost at O(n) per call / O(n^2) total — matching
        -- every other per-comment or per-declaration site in this pipeline,
        -- not worse.
        --
        -- First: try to find an existing OriginalRows containing 'row'
        searchResult =
            Array.foldl
                (\node acc ->
                    when acc.found is
                        Just _ ->
                            { acc | after = Builder.pushLast node acc.after }

                        Nothing ->
                            when lpnBox node is
                                OriginalRows { first = first, last = last } ->
                                    if first <= row && row <= last then
                                        { acc | found = Just node }

                                    else
                                        { acc | before = Builder.pushLast node acc.before }

                                _ ->
                                    { acc | before = Builder.pushLast node acc.before }
                )
                { before = Builder.empty (Array.length nodes), after = Builder.empty 0, found = Nothing }
                nodes
    in
    when searchResult.found is
        Just existing ->
            { origRowNode = existing
            , before = Builder.toArray searchResult.before
            , after = Builder.toArray searchResult.after
            , created = False
            }

        Nothing ->
            -- Own-line comment below a top-level declaration: always detach to a
            -- fresh column-1 OriginalRows, matching elm-format. (An earlier rule,
            -- `columnClaim`, could claim such a comment as the construct's
            -- trailing comment and place it by column depth; that was the source
            -- of the trailing-comment drift/oscillation — the claim and the
            -- placement had to agree about a column that the formatting itself
            -- moved. Detaching everything is both the fix and full elm-format
            -- parity.)
            let
                -- Create a new OriginalRows node inserted in sorted order
                newNode =
                    lpnLeaf (OriginalRows { first = row, last = row, stype = stype })

                insertResult =
                    Array.foldl
                        (\node acc ->
                            if acc.done then
                                { acc | after = Builder.pushLast node acc.after }

                            else
                                when lpnBox node is
                                    OriginalRows { first = first } ->
                                        if row < first then
                                            { acc | after = Builder.pushLast node acc.after, done = True }

                                        else
                                            { acc | before = Builder.pushLast node acc.before }

                                    _ ->
                                        { acc | before = Builder.pushLast node acc.before }
                        )
                        { before = Builder.empty (Array.length nodes), after = Builder.empty 0, done = False }
                        nodes
            in
            { origRowNode = newNode
            , before = Builder.toArray insertResult.before
            , after = Builder.toArray insertResult.after
            , created = True
            }


-- PHASE 2: DESCEND INTO THE OWNING SUBTREE
--
-- Walk down inside the chosen slot, at each level picking the deepest child that
-- owns the comment (childOwnsComment), and insert it among that node's children.
-- Enforces the inner half of the trailing-comment boundary rule.


{-
   Walk down the subtree to find the deepest node that contains the row,
   then insert the comment among that node's children.
   Mirrors the current findDeepestParent + insertAmongChildren logic.
-}
insertCommentIntoSubtree : LPNode -> Int -> Int -> LPNode -> LPNode
insertCommentIntoSubtree commentNode row col node =
    let
        children =
            lpnChildren node

        -- A child only "owns" the comment if no LATER sibling has its
        -- first positioned leaf at or before the comment's position. If a
        -- later sibling does, this child's apparent row-range overlap is a
        -- false positive (e.g. two record types straddling a `->`: the
        -- comment between them appears to be on the same row as the first
        -- record's content, but really belongs between the two records).
        anyLaterSiblingStartsAtOrBefore startIndex =
            Array.foldl
                (\sib acc ->
                    if acc.index >= startIndex then
                        if acc.found then
                            { acc | index = acc.index + 1 }

                        else
                            when lpnFirstPos sib is
                                Nothing ->
                                    { acc | index = acc.index + 1 }

                                Just pos ->
                                    if pos.row < row || (pos.row == row && pos.col <= col) then
                                        { found = True, index = acc.index + 1 }

                                    else
                                        { acc | index = acc.index + 1 }

                    else
                        { acc | index = acc.index + 1 }
                )
                { found = False, index = 0 }
                children
                |> .found

        -- Try to find the first child that has its own children and contains the row
        result =
            Array.foldl
                (\child acc ->
                    if acc.done then
                        { acc | children = Array.pushLast child acc.children, index = acc.index + 1 }

                    else
                        let
                            childChildren =
                                lpnChildren child

                            laterSiblingClaims =
                                anyLaterSiblingStartsAtOrBefore (acc.index + 1)

                            -- True when the next sibling is a *flow boundary* — the
                            -- position-less `in` of a `let … in`, the position-less
                            -- `else` of an `if`, the next `let` binding
                            -- (`IndentedBlock`), or the next `when` branch
                            -- (`WhenBranch`). In each case `child` is a non-last
                            -- binding/branch and a comment trailing its body must NOT
                            -- descend into it: on the first format it would render
                            -- own-line at `child`'s deep indent, but on reparse it
                            -- sits past `child`'s row range and re-attaches at the
                            -- enclosing level — the indent oscillates. Refusing
                            -- descent leaves it a sibling at the current flow level
                            -- (after `in`, leading the else-body, between bindings,
                            -- between branches), which is the stable placement.
                            --
                            -- These sibling kinds are exactly the "new flow
                            -- item" markers; the test is deliberately NOT "any
                            -- following sibling" — a container followed by a
                            -- *continuation* (e.g. a record type before its `->` in a
                            -- signature) has a following sibling too, but a comment
                            -- before that record's `}` belongs inside it, so widening
                            -- the test there reintroduces non-idempotency.
                            nextSiblingIsBoundary =
                                when Array.get (acc.index + 1) children is
                                    Just sib ->
                                        when lpnBox sib is
                                            IndentedBlock ->
                                                True

                                            WhenBranch ->
                                                True

                                            SynthesizedText value ->
                                                value == "in" || value == "else"

                                            _ ->
                                                False

                                    Nothing ->
                                        False

                            -- `child` has a body the comment could live in: it has
                            -- children, or it is an empty bracket the comment sits
                            -- between (`{ {- c -} }`).
                            childHasBody =
                                not (Array.isEmpty childChildren) || commentInsideEmptyBracket child row col

                            hasNoFollowingSibling =
                                when Array.get (acc.index + 1) children is
                                    Nothing ->
                                        True

                                    Just _ ->
                                        False

                            -- The comment merely *trails* `child`'s last token and
                            -- must stay OUTSIDE it: `child` is a boundary-/bracket-
                            -- keeping box (or the next sibling starts a new flow
                            -- item) and the comment isn't genuinely inside child's
                            -- closing bracket. See the trailing-comment boundary rule.
                            -- The comment sits past the LAST thing a paren wraps,
                            -- with only the `)` after it. It belongs to the paren,
                            -- not to whatever that last thing happens to be: the
                            -- reparse reads it that way (its row is past the
                            -- content's, and the `)` is what follows), so
                            -- descending here renders it at the content's own
                            -- indent and the second format pulls it back out to
                            -- the paren's. `(\a ->` ⏎ `body {- c` ⏎ `second -}` ⏎
                            -- `)` oscillated exactly that way, between the lambda
                            -- body's indent and the paren's.
                            -- A bracketed container answers the same way, and for
                            -- the same reason: its `}`/`]`/`)` renders after every
                            -- item, so a comment that brings its own rows renders
                            -- BELOW the item it trails and the reparse reads it as
                            -- the container's, not that item's.
                            --
                            --     { fld = \q -> fn q {- c        first format:
                            --        second -}                       fn q
                            --     }                                  {- c            <- item's indent
                            --                                           second -}
                            --
                            -- and the reparse, seeing a comment on its own row past
                            -- the field, hands it to the record. `AcrossOrVertical`
                            -- is already on `boxKeepsTrailingCommentOutside`, which
                            -- is why the identical shape with NO lambda
                            -- (`{ fld = fn q {- c` ⏎ `second -} }`) has always been
                            -- stable: a lambda is what makes the field render as an
                            -- `IndentedBlock`, and nothing was guarding that.
                            -- **Look for the container that already agrees**: this
                            -- lands the lambda form on the non-lambda form's own
                            -- output, glued after the item's last row.
                            --
                            -- No `hasNoFollowingSibling` here, unlike the paren arm:
                            -- a comment past a NON-last item oscillates the same way
                            -- (`[ \q -> fn q {- c` ⏎ `second -}` ⏎ `, other ]`), and
                            -- at the container level it simply sits between the two
                            -- items, which is what the reparse derives.
                            containerTailKeepsCommentOutside =
                                commentBringsOwnRows
                                    && (when lpnBox node is
                                            ParenBlock _ ->
                                                hasNoFollowingSibling

                                            AllAcrossOrAllVertical _ ->
                                                True

                                            AlwaysVertical _ ->
                                                True

                                            RecordUpdate _ ->
                                                True

                                            _ ->
                                                False
                                       )

                            -- Scoped to a comment that cannot ride the content's
                            -- last row. A single-line `{- c -}` and a `--` both
                            -- stay ON that row (`(0 * f -- k` ⏎ `)`, `r {-c92-}`),
                            -- so they are the content's, and pulling them out to
                            -- the paren would put them on a row of their own — a
                            -- change to shapes that are not broken. Only a
                            -- multi-line block comment brings its own rows and so
                            -- renders below the content whatever owns it.
                            commentBringsOwnRows =
                                when lpnBox commentNode is
                                    BlockComment { loc } ->
                                        String.contains "\n" loc.value

                                    _ ->
                                        False

                            -- `nextSiblingIsBoundary` asked of `child` itself. An
                            -- `IndentedBlock` closes with a DEDENT, not a
                            -- delimiter, so a comment that brings its own rows has
                            -- nothing after it to stay inside of: it renders below
                            -- the block's content at the block's own indent, while
                            -- the reparse — the comment's row now being past the
                            -- block's — hands it to the enclosing flow. That is the
                            -- oscillation `nextSiblingIsBoundary`'s comment
                            -- describes, reached from the other side.
                            --
                            --     else                       first format:
                            --         String.join " " {- c        String.join " "
                            --    second -} [ a, b ]              {- c              <- block's indent
                            --                                       second -}
                            --                                    [ a, b ]
                            --
                            -- and the reparse, seeing the comment on its own row
                            -- past the `else` body and before the array, hands it to
                            -- the call flow they are both items of.
                            --
                            -- Scoped by `commentBringsOwnRows` for the same reason
                            -- `containerTailKeepsCommentOutside` is: a single-line
                            -- `{- c -}` and a `--` both ride the content's last row,
                            -- so they are genuinely the block's and pulling them out
                            -- would give them a row of their own — a change to
                            -- shapes that are not broken.
                            --
                            -- And scoped to a block that something FOLLOWS, which is
                            -- the `ParenBlock` arm above with the sign reversed. A
                            -- paren's comment escapes only when nothing follows it,
                            -- because the `)` is then what renders after the comment;
                            -- a block has no closer at all, so when nothing follows
                            -- it there is nothing to render below the comment, the
                            -- reparse re-derives the same owner, and gluing is
                            -- already the stable answer — `WhenLastBranchTrailingMultiline`
                            -- and `WhenBranchBracketBodyTrailingComment` are exactly
                            -- that shape and both say so.
                            blockTailKeepsCommentOutside =
                                commentBringsOwnRows
                                    && not hasNoFollowingSibling
                                    && (when lpnBox child is
                                            IndentedBlock ->
                                                True

                                            _ ->
                                                False
                                       )

                            trailingCommentEscapes =
                                (boxKeepsTrailingCommentOutside child
                                    || nextSiblingIsBoundary
                                    || containerTailKeepsCommentOutside
                                    || blockTailKeepsCommentOutside
                                )
                                    && subtreeEndsBefore child row col
                                    && not (commentInsideTrailingBracket child row col)

                            -- An elastic container with nothing after it (the
                            -- module header's `exposing ( … )`) owns every comment
                            -- that got this far, on any row — its derived `)` has
                            -- no authored row to be before or after, and it grows
                            -- to sit below whatever is placed inside. Row
                            -- containment alone would keep a comment the author
                            -- wrote on the row BELOW a flat list outside it, where
                            -- it renders glued onto the header line — and reparsing
                            -- that puts it on the list's row, back inside. Same
                            -- reasoning as `commentInsideTrailingBracket`'s elastic
                            -- arm, which is what decides the side once we descend.
                            elasticTailOwnsComment =
                                lpnBracketEndElastic child && hasNoFollowingSibling

                            -- The comment sits between `child`'s OPENING bracket
                            -- and its first item: `[ {- c -} 1, 2 ]`. The bracket
                            -- has no leaf of its own, so the subtree looks like it
                            -- starts at the first item and the comment looks like
                            -- it comes *before* the container — which is why the
                            -- row/`subtreeStartsAfter` tests below both reject it.
                            -- The recorded opening position (`lpnBracketStart`,
                            -- set by `authoredBracketList`) is what says otherwise.
                            --
                            -- Unlike the position-less delimiters (`,` `=` `|`, the
                            -- keywords), the bracket's own position IS in the AST,
                            -- so "inside or outside the container" is a fact we
                            -- have rather than a choice we make. Without this the
                            -- comment was hoisted onto its own line above the whole
                            -- container.
                            commentAfterBracketOpen =
                                when lpnBracketStart child is
                                    Nothing ->
                                        False

                                    Just open ->
                                        posAtOrBefore open row col
                                            && commentInsideTrailingBracket child row col

                            -- `child` owns the comment when its subtree covers the
                            -- comment's row, it doesn't start after the comment, no
                            -- later sibling claims it, and it isn't a trailing comment
                            -- that must stay a sibling.
                            childOwnsComment =
                                childHasBody
                                    && (((subtreeContainsRow child row || elasticTailOwnsComment)
                                            && not (subtreeStartsAfter child row col)
                                        )
                                            || commentAfterBracketOpen
                                       )
                                    && not laterSiblingClaims
                                    && not trailingCommentEscapes
                        in
                        if childOwnsComment then
                            let
                                updatedChild =
                                    insertCommentIntoSubtree commentNode row col child
                            in
                            { children = Array.pushLast updatedChild acc.children
                            , done = True
                            , index = acc.index + 1
                            }

                        else
                            { acc | children = Array.pushLast child acc.children, index = acc.index + 1 }
                )
                { children = [], done = False, index = 0 }
                children
    in
    let
        -- The comment is landing somewhere in this node's subtree, so an
        -- elastic closing bracket here has to move below it: the `)` renders
        -- under whatever the container now holds. Without this the derived
        -- close stays at the last *item*, and the next comment of a trailing
        -- chain reads as past the list and escapes the declaration entirely.
        -- (A no-op on every non-elastic node — see `lpnExtendElasticBracket`.)
        grown =
            lpnExtendElasticBracket (lastRowInSubtree commentNode)
    in
    if result.done then
        grown (lpnReplaceChildren node result.children)

    else
        -- No matching child found; insert at this level
        grown
            (lpnReplaceChildren node
                (insertAmongChildren (lpnBox node) commentNode row col children
                    |> repairTornGapRun (lpnBox node)
                )
            )


{-| Re-decide any `LeadsNext` sibling now that the comment after it exists.

**Comments are attached one at a time, in source order, so a comment is
classified before the rest of its gap-run has been inserted.**
`gapRunCrossesTogether` — rule C1's "the run moves together", asked of the
unrecorded `,` / `|` that rule C2 sends a comment across — therefore sees only
the members written EARLIER. That half works on its own; the other half cannot,
and it is the one that reverses the author's order:

    [ 1 {- a -} -- b        `{- a -}` crosses (nothing follows it yet), then
    , 2                     `-- b` stays, and the two come out CROSSED as
                            `[ 1 -- b` / `, {- a -} 2`.

So the moment a comment lands next to an earlier one that crossed, that earlier
decision is re-taken — by `classifyCommentKind` itself, over the children array
it is being spliced into, which now holds the whole run. Only comments still
holding `LeadsNext` are re-asked, so this cannot disturb a role that was already
final, and re-running it on an unchanged neighbourhood returns what it returned
before.

**Re-classifying rather than rewriting the role is the point.** The fallback a
blocked crossing takes is not one role: `{- a -}` becomes `RidesInline` where it
can share the previous item's line and `TrailsPrevious` where it cannot, and
that choice is made by the branch above `leadsAcrossItemSeparator`. Naming a
replacement here would be a second copy of it — the failure mode
`feedback_fallback_layout_consistency` records, where a fallback that is not
literally the same call silently diverges from the path it stands in for. -}
repairTornGapRun : LPBox -> Array LPNode -> Array LPNode
repairTornGapRun containerBox children =
    let
        reclassifyAt index child =
            if not (commentLeadsNext child) then
                child

            else
                let
                    before =
                        Array.slice 0 index children

                    after =
                        Array.slice (index + 1) (Array.length children) children
                in
                lpnSetCommentRole
                    (classifyCommentKind
                        containerBox
                        child
                        before
                        after
                        (firstRowInSubtree child)
                    )
                    child
    in
    Array.indexedMap reclassifyAt children


{-
   Determines the insertion index by counting how many children end before
   (row, col), then applies the IndentedBlock heuristic: if the next sibling
   is an IndentedBlock, redirect the comment as its first child so it renders
   at IndentedBlock indentation.
-}
insertAmongChildren : LPBox -> LPNode -> Int -> Int -> Array LPNode -> Array LPNode
insertAmongChildren containerBox commentNode row col children =
    if Array.isEmpty children then
        -- No siblings to classify against. A comment inside a bracket container
        -- (`{ {- c -} }`) classifies via the bracket branch (leads its own line);
        -- otherwise keep the role the caller pre-set (`Standalone` for a fresh
        -- top-level slot, else the `LeadsOwnLine` default).
        [ if isBracketContainerBox containerBox then
            lpnSetCommentRole (classifyCommentKind containerBox commentNode [] [] row) commentNode

          else
            commentNode
        ]

    else
        let
            insertionIndex =
                let
                    result =
                        Array.foldl
                            (\child acc ->
                                if subtreeEndsBefore child row col then
                                    { arrayIndex = acc.arrayIndex + 1, insertAt = acc.arrayIndex + 1 }

                                else
                                    { acc | arrayIndex = acc.arrayIndex + 1 }
                            )
                            { arrayIndex = 0, insertAt = 0 }
                            children
                in
                result.insertAt

            { before, after } =
                splitAt insertionIndex children

            -- Advance past any synthesized (position-less) nodes at the boundary.
            -- This prevents comments from being inserted between a pattern and its
            -- synthesized -> arrow, or between other synthesized tokens that logically
            -- belong with the preceding real token.
            --
            -- This is also what carries a comment trailing the LAST `let` binding
            -- PAST the position-less `in` and down to the body column. That is a
            -- DELIBERATE, forced divergence from elm-format (which keeps it with
            -- the bindings), not a coverage gap to close: `in` has no source
            -- position, so before-`in` and after-`in` comments are
            -- indistinguishable, and routing below `in` is the only stable-AND-
            -- correct rule. Scoping this skip to keep the comment with the
            -- bindings makes it OSCILLATE (verified: same-row → own-line at the
            -- binding indent → reparses as no-longer-same-row → back to the body
            -- column). See README divergence #22 and the `LetInTrailingComment`
            -- test named "…not own-line, which would oscillate".
            --
            -- "Position-less" is judged by `lpnLastPos`, with one
            -- exception: a `BodyBlock` is never skipped. A BodyBlock wraps a
            -- declaration's (or `let` binding's) VALUE; when that value is an
            -- empty bracket (`[]` / `{}`) the BodyBlock has a real opening
            -- position but a `Nothing` LAST position (no positioned children),
            -- so `lpnLastPos` alone treats it as skippable and carries a
            -- leading binding comment (`v = {- note -}`) PAST the empty value to
            -- render `[] {- note -}`. If that value later holds an inside comment
            -- it becomes non-empty and absorbs the stranded comment on reparse
            -- (oscillating). Guarding on `BodyBlock` — rather than switching to
            -- `lpnFirstPos` — keeps an empty bracket used as a LIST ITEM
            -- (`[ {}, … ]`, a bare `EmptyBracketed`) skippable as before, so its
            -- own comment placement is unchanged; only the value-wrapper stops
            -- the skip.
            { before = skippedBefore, after = skippedAfter } =
                let
                    helper b a =
                        when Array.popFirst a is
                            Nothing ->
                                { before = b, after = a }

                            Just { first = nextNode, rest = restOfAfter } ->
                                when lpnBox nextNode is
                                    BodyBlock ->
                                        { before = b, after = a }

                                    _ ->
                                        when lpnLastPos nextNode is
                                            Nothing ->
                                                helper (Array.pushLast nextNode b) restOfAfter

                                            Just _ ->
                                                { before = b, after = a }
                in
                helper before after

            -- …and step BACK over a type's `->`, for the one comment shape that
            -- keeps the row it was written on. See
            -- `commentKeepsRowAtTypeArrow`.
            { before = finalBefore, after = finalAfter } =
                if commentKeepsRowAtTypeArrow commentNode skippedBefore then
                    when Array.popLast skippedBefore is
                        Just { last = arrowNode, initial = front } ->
                            { before = front, after = Array.pushFirst arrowNode skippedAfter }

                        Nothing ->
                            { before = skippedBefore, after = skippedAfter }

                else
                    { before = skippedBefore, after = skippedAfter }
        in
        let
            -- The comment's role, decided here from its final neighbours (the
            -- pristine parse rows are still valid at this splice point). A
            -- comment redirected *into* an `IndentedBlock` / `PipelineStep`
            -- leads that body on its own line (`LeadsOwnLine`); everything else
            -- is classified by `classifyCommentKind`.
            classifiedRole =
                classifyCommentKind containerBox commentNode finalBefore finalAfter row

            classifiedComment =
                lpnSetCommentRole classifiedRole commentNode

            leadingComment =
                lpnSetCommentRole LeadsOwnLine commentNode
        in
        when Array.popFirst finalAfter is
            Just { first = nextNode, rest = afterRest } ->
                when lpnBox nextNode is
                    IndentedBlock ->
                        -- Redirect into the IndentedBlock as its first child,
                        -- where it leads that body on a line of its own — a
                        -- `let` binding's value, a `when` branch's body.
                        --
                        -- Not inside a BRACKET, where the same box is an ITEM:
                        -- `folderInsertRecordField` builds a field holding a
                        -- lambda as an `IndentedBlock`, and an item does not
                        -- start its own line — it renders after the container's
                        -- `, `. This is the `BodyBlock` arm's premise
                        -- (`16a9b2e`) one constructor over: "the body always
                        -- starts a line of its own" is a fact about the
                        -- CONTAINER, not about the box. A comment in the `,` gap
                        -- classifies `LeadsNext` and renders `, {- c -} fld =`
                        -- for every other field shape; redirected inside a
                        -- lambda field it became that field's first child, where
                        -- nothing precedes it, so it stacked above `fld =` — and
                        -- the reparse, seeing it alone on the row after the `,`,
                        -- read it as own-line and moved it in front of the
                        -- separator, for ever.
                        --
                        -- Unconditional inside a bracket, including
                        -- `LeadsOwnLine`: there the comment renders at the
                        -- BRACKET's column, above the `,` — which is what the
                        -- same comment before a non-lambda field has always
                        -- done. Redirected inside, it came out glued after the
                        -- `, ` instead, and that shape reparses to the twin's,
                        -- so this authoring oscillated too.
                        if isBracketContainerBox containerBox then
                            finalBefore ++ [ classifiedComment ] ++ finalAfter

                        else
                            let
                                newNextNode =
                                    lpnNode IndentedBlock (Array.pushFirst leadingComment (lpnChildren nextNode))
                            in
                            finalBefore ++ [ newNextNode ] ++ afterRest

                    PipelineStep ->
                        -- Redirect into the PipelineStep as its first child —
                        -- UNLESS the comment can ride the row it was written on.
                        --
                        -- A pipeline step is not like the three unconditional
                        -- wrappers: `seed |> f |> g` stays on one row, so a
                        -- single-line `{- c -}` the author wrote between the seed
                        -- and the operator has a flat line to ride and belongs on
                        -- it (`seed {- c -} |> f`, which is where elm-format puts
                        -- it and where a reparse re-derives it from). Redirecting
                        -- it inside made it the step's first child, where nothing
                        -- precedes it, so it classified `LeadsOwnLine` and forced
                        -- the whole chain vertical — a comment creating line
                        -- breaks the code never needed. Same reasoning as the
                        -- `SoftIndentedBlock` arm below, and the same test: only
                        -- a `RidesInline` comment stays outside; a `--` (which
                        -- ends its line) or a multi-line `{- … -}` breaks the row
                        -- regardless, so it leads the step body as before.
                        if classifiedRole == RidesInline then
                            finalBefore ++ [ classifiedComment ] ++ finalAfter

                        else
                            let
                                newNextNode =
                                    lpnNode PipelineStep (Array.pushFirst leadingComment (lpnChildren nextNode))
                            in
                            finalBefore ++ [ newNextNode ] ++ afterRest

                    BodyBlock ->
                        -- A `BodyBlock` under `OriginalRows` wraps a declaration's
                        -- VALUE. A comment the author wrote on its OWN line
                        -- between `=` and the value belongs to the value, not to
                        -- the head — so redirect it inside, exactly as the
                        -- `IndentedBlock` arm above does for a `let` binding's
                        -- value (which is why the identical comment under a `let`
                        -- binding's `=` already rendered own-line while a
                        -- top-level `x =` glued it onto the head as
                        -- `x = {- c -}`).
                        --
                        -- Unconditional THERE, like those two arms, and for the
                        -- same reason: a declaration's value always starts its own
                        -- line. There is no flat line for a comment to ride, and
                        -- the row the author wrote it on cannot change that —
                        -- `x = {- c -}` and `x =` ⏎ `{- c -}` are the same layout
                        -- question, and elm-format answers both with the comment
                        -- on its own line above the body.
                        --
                        -- Everywhere else `BodyBlock` is a plain grouping wrapper
                        -- around a multi-node value — an array item, a `<|`/`|>`
                        -- step body, a call argument — and it renders through
                        -- `buildFlowBox`, with NO hard newline. `[ \q -> q + one ]`
                        -- is one row, so the premise above is simply false there,
                        -- and applying it anyway let a single-line `{- c -}` break
                        -- the container open (`[ {- c -}` ⏎ `\q -> q + one` ⏎ `]`)
                        -- — rule C3, a comment never forces a break. It reached
                        -- only lambdas, since a lambda is the multi-node value
                        -- that gets this wrapper while `one` / `{ a = 1 }` / `fn x`
                        -- stay bare children and rode the row correctly.
                        --
                        -- Same test as the `PipelineStep` arm above, for the same
                        -- reason: only a `RidesInline` comment has a flat row to
                        -- stay on. A `--` ends its row and a multi-line `{- … -}`
                        -- brings its own newlines, so both still lead the body
                        -- from inside, and an own-row comment classifies
                        -- `LeadsOwnLine` and is redirected as before.
                        if isDeclValueContainer containerBox || classifiedRole /= RidesInline then
                            let
                                newNextNode =
                                    lpnNode BodyBlock (Array.pushFirst leadingComment (lpnChildren nextNode))
                            in
                            finalBefore ++ [ newNextNode ] ++ afterRest

                        else
                            finalBefore ++ [ classifiedComment ] ++ finalAfter

                    SoftIndentedBlock ->
                        -- Unlike the three wrappers above, a `SoftIndentedBlock`
                        -- CAN share the head's row — it is a lambda body the
                        -- author started on the `->` row — so a `{- c -}` before
                        -- it legitimately rides that row, and the render side's
                        -- `pairLeadingComments` carries the comment down with the
                        -- body if the body turns out to wrap. That is a fixed
                        -- point, and this arm must not disturb it.
                        --
                        -- A `--` is different, and only in one way that decides
                        -- everything: it ends its line. The body is therefore
                        -- forced onto the next row no matter what, and a reparse
                        -- reads a body starting on a later row as an
                        -- `IndentedBlock` — whose arm above puts the comment
                        -- INSIDE it. So leaving a `--` outside emits a shape that
                        -- reparses into the other structure and moves:
                        -- `\q -> -- c` / `{ … }` became `\q ->` / `-- c` /
                        -- `{ … }` on the second format. Redirecting it here makes
                        -- the first format land on the shape every later one
                        -- settles on, and matches what the `IndentedBlock` arm
                        -- already does for the identical comment written one row
                        -- lower.
                        when lpnBox commentNode is
                            SingleLineComment _ ->
                                let
                                    newNextNode =
                                        lpnNode SoftIndentedBlock (Array.pushFirst leadingComment (lpnChildren nextNode))
                                in
                                finalBefore ++ [ newNextNode ] ++ afterRest

                            _ ->
                                finalBefore ++ [ classifiedComment ] ++ finalAfter

                    _ ->
                        finalBefore ++ [ classifiedComment ] ++ finalAfter

            Nothing ->
                finalBefore ++ [ classifiedComment ]


-- PREDICATES: POSITION & BOX-KIND TESTS
--
-- Shared by both phases. The position tests (subtreeContainsRow, subtreeEndsBefore,
-- subtreeStartsAfter, posAtOrBefore, lpnFirstPos/lpnLastPos)
-- read the O(1) bounds cache; the box classifiers (boxKeepsTrailingCommentOutside,
-- commentInside*, isBracketContainerBox) decide whether a trailing comment stays
-- outside a container or descends into it.


-- Box-type guards for the descent. These predicates name the LPBox kinds that a
-- trailing comment must never be sucked *into*, even when `child` is the last
-- True for box kinds that, when a comment sits past their last token, must keep
-- that trailing comment OUTSIDE themselves — it belongs beside them, not inside.
-- The node is the last in its flow (no following sibling to fall beside), or the
-- descent guard pairs this with `nextSiblingIsBoundary` for a mid-flow node that
-- still starts a new flow item. A comment genuinely inside a glued/bracketed
-- form still descends, via `commentInsideTrailingBracket` /
-- `commentInsideEmptyBracket`.
--
-- This is the single declared list of "never swallow a trailing comment" box
-- kinds — to teach a new construct the same rule, add it here rather than
-- threading another predicate into the guard.
--
-- The exception is a kind that keeps the comment out only for SOME comments or
-- only in some positions, which this list cannot express: it is asked of every
-- comment kind, in every position, and answers with the box alone. The descent
-- guard therefore has two scoped siblings, and a new rule belongs in one of
-- them only if it needs a scope this list cannot carry —
-- `containerTailKeepsCommentOutside` (a bracketed container, but only for a
-- comment that brings its own rows) and `blockTailKeepsCommentOutside` (an
-- `IndentedBlock`, and then only when something follows it). Adding either kind
-- here unscoped was tried: `IndentedBlock` alone breaks the two `when`-last-branch
-- fixtures, whose trailing comment must stay glued.
--
-- Per kind:
--   AcrossOrVertical                       — function-call / argument flow
--   IfCondition                            — `if` / `else if` condition
--   PrefixGlue                             — `\`-glued lambda head or unary `-`;
--       matters even mid-flow, where the next lambda parameter's flow separator
--       would otherwise append the comment with a leading space and shift that
--       parameter by a column (non-idempotent)
--   ParenBlock                             — parenthesised expression
--   AllAcrossOrAllVertical / AlwaysVertical — bracketed item list (`{ a, b }`,
--       `[ x, y ]`, exposing lists): a comment past the closing bracket is a
--       sibling, not an item.
--   RecordUpdate                           — `{ base | f = v }` and the
--       extensible record TYPE `{ r | x : Int }`: same rule as the bracket
--       lists. Its close is EXACT (`lpnBracketNode locExpr.end`), so
--       `commentInsideTrailingBracket` still lets a comment written *before*
--       the `}` descend to the last field; only one written past the `}`
--       stays outside. Excluding it used to let `fn a { r | a = 1 } {- c -} b`
--       render as `{ r | a = 1 {- c -} }`, i.e. as a comment on the last
--       field rather than on the record.
-- For every *other* box kind the sibling check in the descent expresses the same
-- intent whenever a sibling follows, so most constructs need no entry here.
boxKeepsTrailingCommentOutside : LPNode -> Bool
boxKeepsTrailingCommentOutside node =
    when lpnBox node is
        AcrossOrVertical _ ->
            True

        RecordUpdate _ ->
            True

        IfCondition _ ->
            True

        PrefixGlue _ ->
            True

        ParenBlock _ ->
            True

        AllAcrossOrAllVertical _ ->
            True

        AlwaysVertical _ ->
            True

        _ ->
            False


-- True if the node is an EmptyBracketed literal AND the comment sits *before*
-- its closing bracket — i.e. genuinely between the brackets. We then allow
-- descent so the comment becomes the literal's child and renders inside
-- (`{ {- c -} }` rather than `{} {- c -}`). A comment past the closing bracket
-- (`[] {- c -}`) must NOT be absorbed: doing so flips it to `[ {- c -} ]` on
-- reparse (non-idempotent). The closing position is `loc.end`.
commentInsideEmptyBracket : LPNode -> Int -> Int -> Bool
commentInsideEmptyBracket node row col =
    when lpnBox node is
        EmptyBracketed loc ->
            row < loc.end.row || (row == loc.end.row && col < loc.end.col)

        _ ->
            False


{-| True if the comment is sitting just past the closing bracket of
the rightmost bracket-list inside `node`'s subtree — i.e. the user
plausibly wrote it between the bracket's last item and the closing
bracket character. Allows the descent guard for `AcrossOrVertical`
to admit comments like `\[a, b, c {- trailing -}] -> body`, where the
AcrossOrVertical's last positioned token (`c`) is before the
comment but the bracket genuinely owns it.

The column-proximity threshold (`+2`) accounts for one whitespace
plus the closing bracket character (`]` / `}` / `)`) that we don't
track explicitly in the LPT.

When the bracket node carries its real closing-bracket position (built via
`lpnBracketNode`), a comment on an *earlier* row than the closing bracket is
also "inside": it was written on its own line between the last item and the
closing bracket, e.g.

    { a = 1
    , b = 2
    {- here -}
    }

Without this, such a comment falls past the last item's row range and escapes
the container entirely (non-idempotently).
-}
commentInsideTrailingBracket : LPNode -> Int -> Int -> Bool
commentInsideTrailingBracket node row col =
    when lpnLastBracketEnd node is
        Nothing ->
            False

        Just bracketEnd ->
            if lpnBracketEndElastic node then
                -- An elastic close (the module header's `exposing ( … )`) has
                -- no authored `)` to be before or after: nothing follows the
                -- list inside the declaration, so anything that reached this
                -- node belongs inside it, above the `)`. Deciding by proximity
                -- to the derived close instead is what made a flat list's
                -- result depend on the order the names were written in, and
                -- what let the second link of a trailing comment chain fall
                -- past the list and detach. See `lpnElasticBracketNode`.
                True

            else if lpnBracketEndExact node then
                -- `bracketEnd` is `loc.end`, one column past the closing
                -- bracket. A comment strictly before it is genuinely between
                -- the last item and the bracket (inside); one at or after the
                -- bracket (e.g. `{ a } {- c -}` used as a list item) must stay
                -- outside, else it gets absorbed and the item expands on
                -- reparse (non-idempotent).
                (row < bracketEnd.row)
                    || (bracketEnd.row == row && col < bracketEnd.col)

            else
                -- `bracketEnd` is the last item's end; the real (unrepresented)
                -- closing bracket is a little further right/down. Allow slack.
                (row < bracketEnd.row)
                    || (bracketEnd.row == row && col <= bracketEnd.col + 2)


subtreeContainsRow : LPNode -> Int -> Bool
subtreeContainsRow node row =
    let
        range =
            subtreeRowRange node
    in
    range.maxRow >= 0 && range.minRow <= row && row <= range.maxRow


{-
   posAtOrBefore returns True if the position is on an earlier row, or on
   the same row with an equal or earlier column -- inclusive where
   `posStrictlyBefore` is strict.
-}
posAtOrBefore : Position -> Int -> Int -> Bool
posAtOrBefore pos row col =
    pos.row < row || (pos.row == row && pos.col <= col)


{-
   splitAt is a small helper that divides an array at a given index.
-}
splitAt : Int -> Array a -> { before : Array a, after : Array a }
splitAt index arr =
    Array.foldl
        (\item acc ->
            if acc.count < index then
                { acc
                    | before = Array.pushLast item acc.before
                    , count = acc.count + 1
                }

            else
                { acc | after = Array.pushLast item acc.after }
        )
        { before = [], after = [], count = 0 }
        arr
        |> (\result -> { before = result.before, after = result.after })


subtreeEndsBefore : LPNode -> Int -> Int -> Bool
subtreeEndsBefore node row col =
    lpnLastPos node
        |> (\maybePos ->
                when maybePos is
                    Nothing ->
                        False

                    Just pos ->
                        posAtOrBefore pos row col
           )


-- True if the subtree's first positioned leaf is at or after the given (row, col).
subtreeStartsAfter : LPNode -> Int -> Int -> Bool
subtreeStartsAfter node row col =
    when lpnFirstPos node is
        Nothing ->
            False

        Just pos ->
            pos.row > row || (pos.row == row && pos.col > col)


-- COMMENT-ROLE CLASSIFICATION
--
-- Decide, at the single insertion point above (where the parse rows are still
-- pristine), how a comment joins its neighbours — the `CommentRole` the
-- renderer then reads instead of re-deriving from source rows. This
-- consolidates the rules that today live scattered across the render layer
-- (FlowPolicy's two-row state machine, `lastRenderedRow`, the bracket
-- descent). `classifyCommentKind` below is where each rule is stated, with
-- the fixture that pins it.


-- True for the bracket container box kinds whose interior comments render via
-- the bracket-list path: a comment that is a child of one of these is classified
-- by `classifyCommentKind`'s bracket branch (its permissive same-row rule).
{-| The module line's `exposing ( … )` list. `ListParen` is exclusively an
exposing list (see `SortSymbols.gren`), so this identifies it exactly.

Used by `headerTailGlue` to tell the header's position-less TAIL from its middle.
The module line is built as `[ keyword, name, where…, exposing, exports ]`
(`MakeLogical.processModuleLine`), so a comment with nothing but the `exposing`
keyword and this list after it is on the tail — whatever rows anything is on. -}
isExposingListNode : LPNode -> Bool
isExposingListNode node =
    when lpnBox node is
        AllAcrossOrAllVertical ListParen ->
            True

        AlwaysVertical ListParen ->
            True

        _ ->
            False


isBracketContainerBox : LPBox -> Bool
isBracketContainerBox box =
    when box is
        AllAcrossOrAllVertical _ ->
            True

        AlwaysVertical _ ->
            True

        EmptyBracketed _ ->
            True

        RecordUpdate _ ->
            True

        _ ->
            False


-- True for the container whose `BodyBlock` child is a declaration's VALUE, which
-- always starts a line of its own. Every other parent gives `BodyBlock` the
-- meaning "a value made of several nodes" — an array item, a `<|`/`|>` step body,
-- a call argument — and renders it as a plain flow that can stay on one row. The
-- distinction decides whether a comment before it has a line to ride; see the
-- `BodyBlock` arm of `insertAmongChildren`. (A `let` binding's value is an
-- `IndentedBlock`, handled by its own arm, so it does not need naming here.)
isDeclValueContainer : LPBox -> Bool
isDeclValueContainer box =
    when box is
        OriginalRows _ ->
            True

        _ ->
            False


-- True for the `{ base | field … }` box (record update expression, and the
-- extensible record TYPE, which shares it). Its children are the FIELDS, so
-- "first child" does not mean "written just after the `{`" the way it does for
-- an item list — see the bracket branch of `classifyCommentKind`.
isRecordUpdateBox : LPBox -> Bool
isRecordUpdateBox box =
    when box is
        RecordUpdate _ ->
            True

        _ ->
            False


{-| True when a node or any descendant is a `"""…"""` literal. Such a node's box
cannot take a comment glued to the front of it: the glue shifts the box's first
row by the comment's width, and Gren requires every row of a multi-line string to
be indented equally, so the result stops parsing. -}
subtreeHasMultilineString : LPNode -> Bool
subtreeHasMultilineString node =
    when lpnBox node is
        MultilineString _ ->
            True

        _ ->
            Array.any subtreeHasMultilineString (lpnChildren node)


{-| True when a bracket container is an `exposing ( … )` list. `ListParen` is
used for nothing else (`SortSymbols.gren`: "ListParen is exclusively exposing
lists"), so the box alone says it. -}
isExposingListBox : LPBox -> Bool
isExposingListBox box =
    when box is
        AllAcrossOrAllVertical ListParen ->
            True

        AlwaysVertical ListParen ->
            True

        _ ->
            False


{-| True when a comment is a `{- … -}` the author wrote on one source row — the
only kind that can share a line with code beside it. A `--` runs to end of line
and a multi-line `{- … -}` brings its own newlines.

`NodeClassify.commentTextCanRide` is the same question asked render-side (Gren
forbids the import that would share one definition). Both read the comment's TEXT
only, never its position, which is why the render-side copy does not breach the
render invariant — keep them in step. -}
commentIsSingleLineBlock : LPNode -> Bool
commentIsSingleLineBlock node =
    when lpnBox node is
        BlockComment { loc } ->
            not (String.contains "\n" loc.value)

        _ ->
            False


{-| Would every comment sharing this gap cross the separator too?

**Rule C1 — one gap is one attachment, and the run moves together.** The two
`leadsAcross*Separator` tests below send a comment past an unrecorded `,` / `|`
to the item AFTER it (rule C2), and both ask `commentIsSingleLineBlock` of the
ONE comment being classified. A gap holds a *run*, and the other members answer
that question differently: a `--` and a multi-line `{- … -}` both stay with the
item above. Applying the rule per member therefore tears the run across the
separator — `[ 1 {- a -} -- b` ⏎ `, 2` came out as `[ 1 -- b` ⏎ `, {- a -} 2`,
with the two comments **crossed**, the author's order reversed.

So the movability test is asked of the whole run: it crosses only if every
member would. This is the same all-or-nothing C3 already applies to *riding*
(«the run rides iff every member rides») — the missing half is *moving*. It can
only make a comment stay where it was written, never move one that did not move
before, so a gap holding one comment (and a homogeneous run, which already moved
together) is unaffected.

**Only a mixed run can reach it, which is why nothing here saw it until
2026-08-06.** A run of single-line `{- -}`s all move; a run of `--`s all stay;
`fuzz-idempotency.py --run N` splices N copies of ONE kind and so produces
neither tear. It needed that gate's `--mix` axis. Worse, the reordering oracle
catches only the half where the mover was written FIRST — `{- a\ntwo -} {- b -}`
tears with the output still in source order, and no gate in this repo can see
that at all.

`before` / `after` are the comment's siblings in source order, so the run is the
contiguous comment suffix of the first and the contiguous comment prefix of the
second — a real item between two comments means a different gap. -}
gapRunCrossesTogether : Array LPNode -> Array LPNode -> Bool
gapRunCrossesTogether before after =
    let
        -- `Array.any (not ...)` rather than an `all`: the comment being
        -- classified is excluded from both arrays and tested by the caller.
        anyStays nodes =
            Array.any (\n -> not (commentIsSingleLineBlock n)) nodes

        contiguousComments nodes =
            Array.foldl
                (\n acc ->
                    if acc.stopped || not (isCommentNode n) then
                        { acc | stopped = True }

                    else
                        { acc | run = Array.pushLast n acc.run }
                )
                { run = [], stopped = False }
                nodes
                |> .run
    in
    not (anyStays (contiguousComments (Array.reverse before)))
        && not (anyStays (contiguousComments after))


{-| True when this comment was written BEFORE a record update's base name — the
opener slot, `{ {- c -} rec | a = 1 }` — rather than in the ambiguous gap around
the `|`. The base's start is a real recorded position (`RecordUpdate.name.start`,
the base expression's own start), which is what makes the two tellable apart;
the `|` between them is not recorded, so once past the base nothing more can be
resolved.

Anything that is not a positioned comment leaf, or not a record update, is not
an opener. -}
commentPrecedesUpdateBase : LPBox -> LPNode -> Bool
commentPrecedesUpdateBase containerBox commentNode =
    when { container = containerBox, comment = lpnBox commentNode } is
        { container = RecordUpdate { name }, comment = SingleLineComment { loc } } ->
            posAtOrBefore loc.start name.start.row name.start.col

        { container = RecordUpdate { name }, comment = BlockComment { loc } } ->
            posAtOrBefore loc.start name.start.row name.start.col

        _ ->
            False


-- True when an `AcrossOrVertical` is a union variant (`= Ctor` / `| Ctor`)
-- rather than a call: its first child is the synthesized `=` or `|` head.
isUnionVariantFlow : LPNode -> Bool
isUnionVariantFlow node =
    when Array.first (lpnChildren node) is
        Just c ->
            when lpnBox c is
                SynthesizedText v ->
                    v == "=" || v == "|"

                _ ->
                    False

        Nothing ->
            False


-- The last row a node actually renders content on: the max of its last
-- positioned token and its closing bracket (elm-format's "last rendered line").
-- This is the row a trailing comment must share to glue onto it. (Exactly the
-- `lastRenderedRow` the render layer reconstructs per box kind — computed here
-- once, from the pristine cached bounds.)
lastRenderedRow : LPNode -> Int
lastRenderedRow node =
    let
        lp =
            when lpnLastPos node is
                Just p ->
                    p.row

                Nothing ->
                    -1

        be =
            when lpnLastBracketEnd node is
                Just p ->
                    p.row

                Nothing ->
                    -1
    in
    max lp be


-- True when a comment directly trailing this node glues regardless of row.
-- Scoped to ONE token: the zero-width synthesized signature `->` (an
-- `UnbreakableText "->"` anchored at the end of its `from` type, not where it
-- renders), so a pure row test would flip on reformat. Other position-less tokens (`in`, `=`, `:`) are NOT elided
-- here: a comment on its own row after them legitimately stays own-line (e.g.
-- the `let … in` trailing comment, a deliberate README divergence).
--
-- Reached only by the comment shapes that do NOT keep their row — in practice a
-- single-line `{- -}`, which renders `-> {- c -} String`. A `--` or a multi-line
-- `{- … -}` is stepped back over the arrow by `commentKeepsRowAtTypeArrow`
-- before it is ever classified, so its `prev` is the type to the arrow's left.
nodeIsElided : LPNode -> Bool
nodeIsElided node =
    when lpnBox node is
        UnbreakableText loc ->
            loc.value == "->"
                && loc.start.row == loc.end.row
                && loc.start.col == loc.end.col

        _ ->
            False


{-| Rule **C2**'s line-leading-separator exception at a type's `->`.

The arrow carries no source position — it is anchored zero-width at the end of
the type to its left (`InsertTypes.makeSignatureBranch`) — so every comment
written in the arrow gap sorts *after* it, whichever side of it the author typed
it on. That is the general C2 rule, and for a single-line `{- -}` it stands:
`Int {- c -}` ⏎ `-> String` and `Int` ⏎ `-> {- c -} String` both render
`-> {- c -} String`, the way a `,` gap sends one to the item below.

A `--` and a multi-line `{- … -}` are the exception, the same one `,` and a
union's / a record update's `|` already make: a broken signature renders its
`->` **line-leading**, so a comment above one strands nothing — it just sits at
the arrow's column — and both shapes end their row anyway, so the row the author
gave them is a spelling the formatter can see and keep:

    foo :                       foo :
        Int -- c                    Int
        -> String                   -- c
                                    -> String

Stepping the splice point back over the arrow is all that takes: the comment's
`prev` becomes the type to its left, and the ordinary same-row / own-row rules
then say `TrailsPrevious` or `LeadsOwnLine`. (The third spelling — `Int -> -- c`
⏎ `String`, written past the arrow but still on the left type's row — is
positionally identical to the trailing one, since the arrow is invisible, and
collapses onto it: three ways to type it, two outcomes, exactly as at a `,`.)

Requires something before the arrow, so the step-back can never leave the
comment with no predecessor to classify against. -}
commentKeepsRowAtTypeArrow : LPNode -> Array LPNode -> Bool
commentKeepsRowAtTypeArrow commentNode before =
    when Array.popLast before is
        Just { last = prevNode, initial = front } ->
            nodeIsElided prevNode
                && not (Array.isEmpty front)
                && not (commentIsSingleLineBlock commentNode)

        Nothing ->
            False


-- The exact closing-bracket row of a bracket container (its `]`/`}`/`)`), or -1.
bracketClosingRow : LPNode -> Int
bracketClosingRow node =
    when lpnLastBracketEnd node is
        Just p ->
            p.row

        Nothing ->
            -1


{-| True when the LAST thing a flow renders is a closing bracket — the question
`prevBlockGlueRow`'s `AcrossOrVertical` arm means to ask, and one
`lastBracketEnd` alone cannot answer.

That cache keeps the RIGHTMOST bracket anywhere in the subtree, which for a
lambda head (`\[ 1 ] ->`) is the *pattern's* `]`, with the `->` still to render
after it. So a `{- c -}` written past the arrow found a glue row, classified
`RidesInline`, and glued onto the arrow's row while a multi-line body dropped
below it.

**The reparse cannot read that shape back.** A body starting on a later row
arrives as an `IndentedBlock`, not a `SoftIndentedBlock`, and
`insertAmongChildren`'s `IndentedBlock` arm redirects a comment before one
INSIDE it as `LeadsOwnLine` — so the second format stacked it above the body and
the file never settled. That arm's `SoftIndentedBlock` neighbour already
documents this exact trade for a `--`, which is redirected for the same reason;
this is the single-line `{- -}` whose body turns out to render multi-line,
reaching it one comment kind over. Holding the arrow's row is therefore not
available without changing that redirect, and elm-format does not hold it
either (it breaks a lambda body whenever a comment follows the `->`, even one
that fits).

`\y -> {- c -}`, with no bracket in the pattern, had no glue row and was a fixed
point all along; this makes every lambda head agree with it, in every container
(record field, record update field, `let` binding, list item, paren argument,
declaration value — the whole family oscillated). A single-line body still rides
the arrow's row either way: that is `literalCommentsRideFlatLine` reading the
comment's SHAPE, which no role decides — so C3 holds, the comment forces no
break, and C7 is untouched (it governs a run's rows against each other, and a
run written on the arrow's row still stays on one row).

Structural rather than positional because the token that follows the bracket is
exactly the kind that carries no position (`SynthesizedText "->"`, `"="`), so
comparing `lastBracketEnd` against the flow's last recorded position cannot see
it. The zero-width signature `->` is skipped: it renders nothing, so a flow
ending in it still ends at its bracket.
-}
flowEndsAtBracketClose : LPNode -> Bool
flowEndsAtBracketClose node =
    when lpnBox node is
        AllAcrossOrAllVertical _ ->
            True

        AlwaysVertical _ ->
            True

        EmptyBracketed _ ->
            True

        -- A paren/record update closes with its own `)`/`}` (both are built via
        -- `lpnBracketNode`), so reaching one is reaching a close too.
        ParenBlock _ ->
            True

        RecordUpdate _ ->
            True

        _ ->
            when Array.last (Array.keepIf (\c -> not (nodeIsElided c)) (lpnChildren node)) is
                Just lastChild ->
                    flowEndsAtBracketClose lastChild

                Nothing ->
                    False


-- True if the subtree contains any line/block comment leaf.
subtreeContainsComment : LPNode -> Bool
subtreeContainsComment node =
    isCommentNode node || Array.any subtreeContainsComment (lpnChildren node)


{-| Whether a bracket container renders multi-line, so a following block comment
can glue onto its closing bracket's line.

**The box type IS the answer**, and asking anything else was the bug.
`authoredBracketList` has already made this exact decision once, from
`itemsSpanRows`, and recorded it in the constructor: `AlwaysVertical` renders
vertical, `AllAcrossOrAllVertical` renders inline unless a comment forces it
open. Re-deriving it here from `subtreeRowRange` asked a DIFFERENT question —
"does anything in this subtree span rows", which counts a break *inside* one
item, the very thing `itemsSpanRows` documents itself as ignoring ("A lone item
that is itself multi-line does not count, since there is no gap *between* items
to cross").

The two disagree on exactly the shape that collapses. `[ 1` ⏎ `]` has one item,
so `itemsSpanRows` is False and the box is `AllAcrossOrAllVertical` — it renders
`[ 1 ]`, one row ([#21](../../../docs/elmFormatComparison.md#divergence-21)) —
while the row range still spans the author's two rows. A trailing multi-line
`{- … -}` therefore found a glue row that the output does not have, glued on the
first format, and took its own row on the second, for ever. 54 findings, from
`matrix-syntax.py --comments` once it swept the multi-line kind.

So this now reads the stored flag and no rows at all. That is the same
correction `commentSplitsType` needed and the same one `45f7269` needed: a
placement decided from a source row the formatting itself invalidates. Here the
fix is subtractive — the author-intent flag was already computed, stored and
correct, and the mirror beside it was the only thing wrong.
-}
bracketRendersMultiline : LPNode -> Bool
bracketRendersMultiline node =
    when lpnBox node is
        AlwaysVertical _ ->
            True

        _ ->
            subtreeContainsComment node


{-| True when a `ParenBlock`'s content renders multi-line however the author laid
it out, so the `)` gets a row of its own that a trailing comment can glue onto.

`prevBlockGlueRow`'s `ParenBlock` arm used the source row span alone as its
"does this paren close on a `)` row a comment can glue onto?" test. A paren the
author wrote on ONE row but whose content forces a break defeats that proxy: the
comment classifies `LeadsOwnLine`, and at the tail of a declaration that renders
it on a fresh row BELOW the declaration's last token — a row the reparse can
never attribute back to the declaration, so it re-homes as a top-level
`Standalone` comment at column 1. `0 <| ( if 0 then 1 else 2 ) {- c -}` moved
indented ⟶ col 1 on the second format.

Only the backward-pipeline path exposed it: `|>` peels and glues its step's
trailing comments without consulting the role, a binop container classifies by
row comparison instead of a glue row, and a call flow reaches the paren through
`AcrossOrVertical`'s `bracketClosingRow` arm.

This reads the flag `insertParens` stored, rather than re-deriving the answer
here. It used to dispatch on the paren's FIRST CHILD box — `WhenFlow` /
`IfCondition` / a `let` head — which is only the shape where the breaking
construct is the whole content. It is not where the construct has to be:
`0 <| ( f (if a then b else c) ) {- c -}` buries the `if` under a call, and
`0 <| ( 0 |> 0 <| 0 ) {- c -}` breaks with no block construct at all — a mixed
`|>`/`<|` chain always renders one step per line. Both moved indented ⟶ col 1
on the second format, the same oscillation the first-child test was added to
close; the second is what `fuzzrun.py` seed 10257116 found. `exprAlwaysBreaks`
is the closed full-tree recursion (it already answers this question for a lambda
body — see `insertLambda`'s `bodyAlwaysBreaksVertical`), so it subsumes every
buried position instead of one enumerated shape per bug. -}
parenContentAlwaysBreaks : LPNode -> Bool
parenContentAlwaysBreaks node =
    when lpnBox node is
        ParenBlock { contentAlwaysBreaks } ->
            contentAlwaysBreaks

        _ ->
            False


-- The source row a following `--` line comment glues onto (or -1 for "no glue,
-- stay own-line"). A faithful port of `factsFor`'s `startRowLine` per box kind:
-- a `--` is unambiguously trailing, so it glues onto the previous item's last
-- rendered row in strictly more places than a block comment.
prevLineGlueRow : LPNode -> Int
prevLineGlueRow node =
    when lpnBox node is
        UnbreakableText loc ->
            loc.start.row

        EmptyBracketed loc ->
            loc.start.row

        AcrossOrVertical _ ->
            lastRenderedRow node

        ParenBlock _ ->
            lastRenderedRow node

        AllAcrossOrAllVertical _ ->
            bracketClosingRow node

        AlwaysVertical _ ->
            bracketClosingRow node

        -- Same as the other bracketed containers: a `--` past the `}` glues onto
        -- the update's closing-bracket row (so it stays on that line instead of
        -- dropping to its own, which would oscillate).
        RecordUpdate _ ->
            bracketClosingRow node

        WhenBranch ->
            (subtreeRowRange node).maxRow

        -- Always multi-line by construction; a `--` unambiguously glues onto
        -- the closing `"""`'s row (`"""..."""` ⏎ `-- c` stays on that line).
        MultilineString _ ->
            lastRenderedRow node

        -- Unary `-` (`-String.acc -- c`): a `--` is unambiguously trailing,
        -- so it glues onto the negated content's last rendered row same as
        -- any other single-child wrapper (`AcrossOrVertical`/`ParenBlock`
        -- above). Missing this case (falling through to the `_ -> -1`
        -- default) made a same-row `--` after a bare unary-minus body
        -- always classify as `LeadsOwnLine`, oscillating on reparse exactly
        -- like the `MultilineString` gap above once did. A lambda head does
        -- NOT glue (`gluesTrailingComment` is `False` for it) —
        -- `KitchenComments`' fixture-verified behavior keeps a same-row `--`
        -- after a destructured lambda parameter own-line (`\{ a, b }` ⏎
        -- `-- c` ⏎ next-param), so this stays `-1` (own-line) for that
        -- prefix, same as before this case existed at all.
        PrefixGlue loc ->
            if gluesTrailingComment loc.value then
                lastRenderedRow node

            else
                -1

        -- A run of comments: the following one glues onto the previous
        -- comment's LAST row, not its first. For a `--` (always one row) the
        -- two coincide, but a multi-line `{- … -}`'s `-}` can close several
        -- rows below its `{-`, and a same-row successor sits on that close
        -- row (`{- multi⏎line -} {- c -}`). Using the first row misclassified
        -- the successor as `LeadsOwnLine`, which (with the render-side
        -- `TerminatedByBlockComment` policy) oscillated col 4 ↔ col 0.
        SingleLineComment _ ->
            lastRowInSubtree node

        BlockComment _ ->
            lastRowInSubtree node

        _ ->
            -1


-- The source row a following `{- -}` block comment glues onto (or -1). The
-- coarser counterpart of `prevLineGlueRow` (`factsFor`'s `startRow` /
-- `prevRowBlock`): a block comment glues after a bare token or a *multi-line*
-- bracket's close, but NOT after a plain-token call, a lone/empty bracket, or a
-- single-line bracket — there a `--` still glues but a `{- -}` stays own-line.
--
-- `commentIsMultiline` is the trailing comment's OWN shape, not the preceding
-- node's — it overrides the "stays own-line after a single-line bracket"
-- refusal, but ONLY for `AllAcrossOrAllVertical ListParen`. `ListParen` is
-- exclusively an exposing list (`SortSymbols.gren`'s own comment: "ListParen
-- is exclusively exposing lists, so this only ever fires on them" — never a
-- record/array pattern or literal, which use `ListCurly`/`ListSquare`), so
-- this override cannot reach those. A comment that is itself multi-line can
-- never render truly inline no matter what precedes it, so denying it a glue
-- row after a single-line EXPOSING list is never correct: without this
-- override, `import Foo exposing (y) {- multi⏎line -}` classified
-- `LeadsOwnLine`, which renders on a fresh row; reparsing that output then
-- reads the comment as an unattached standalone (its row no longer falls
-- inside the import's own row range) instead of the import's own trailing
-- comment, so the SECOND format drops it back to a different placement — an
-- oscillation only a multi-row comment can trigger, so it was unreachable
-- before comments themselves could span rows (GENERATOR.md v1.25, which
-- found it).
--
-- The SAME override on the general `ListCurly`/`ListSquare` case, or on
-- `ParenBlock` (any parenthesized expression), was tried first and reverted:
-- it fixed the same bug but ALSO changed an already-fixture-verified,
-- deliberately-own-line shape — `{ x } {- multi⏎line -} as whole` (a record
-- PATTERN, `KitchenSink`'s "3-line block comments at every round-tripping
-- position") started gluing onto `{ x }` instead of staying own-line. Only
-- the exposing-list shape was ever actually found broken; scope the fix to
-- exactly that shape rather than the broader "any bracket, any comment"
-- rule the first attempt used, which the fixture suite (not the generator)
-- caught.
prevBlockGlueRow : Bool -> LPNode -> Int
prevBlockGlueRow commentIsMultiline node =
    when lpnBox node is
        UnbreakableText loc ->
            loc.start.row

        AcrossOrVertical _ ->
            if isUnionVariantFlow node then
                -- A union variant (`= Red`, `| Green`) is a bare constructor, not
                -- a call: a same-row block comment rides it (`= Red {- warm -}`),
                -- so the union stays flat. Distinguished by the `=`/`|` head.
                lastRenderedRow node

            else if flowEndsAtBracketClose node then
                -- A call flow: only glue when it ends in a closing bracket
                -- (`foo`⏎`  [ … ]`); after a plain-token call a block comment
                -- stays own-line (`foo bar` ⟶ `{- c -}`). "Ends in" is asked of
                -- the flow's last child, not of the cached rightmost bracket —
                -- see `flowEndsAtBracketClose` for the lambda head that made
                -- the two differ.
                bracketClosingRow node

            else
                -1

        -- A same-row block comment glues onto a multi-line paren's closing `)`
        -- (`(bar baz) {- c -}`); after a single-line paren it stays own-line, as
        -- after a plain call.
        --
        -- "Renders multi-line" is the paren's own stored `forceVertical` — set
        -- when the author wrote the `(` and `)` on different rows, which is what
        -- makes the renderer put the `)` on its own line — plus a paren whose
        -- content breaks unconditionally however the author laid it out
        -- (`parenContentAlwaysBreaks`; see there for the oscillation that
        -- closes).
        --
        -- It used to be the subtree's ROW SPAN, which is a different question
        -- and wrong for the same reason `bracketRendersMultiline`'s was: a
        -- formatter-SYNTHESIZED paren (a type or pattern wrap) carries no author
        -- position, so `forceVertical` is False and it renders flat — while its
        -- content's rows still span whatever the author wrote. `foo : (Int` ⏎
        -- `-> Int) {- … -}` renders `(Int -> Int)` on one row, so the glue row
        -- the span promised does not exist in the output, and the second format
        -- moved the comment to its own row. Roughly 48 of the 54 findings the
        -- multi-line comment sweep turned up were this arm.
        ParenBlock info ->
            if info.forceVertical || parenContentAlwaysBreaks node then
                lastRenderedRow node

            else
                -1

        AllAcrossOrAllVertical ListParen ->
            if bracketRendersMultiline node || commentIsMultiline then
                bracketClosingRow node

            else
                -1

        AllAcrossOrAllVertical _ ->
            if bracketRendersMultiline node then
                bracketClosingRow node

            else
                -1

        AlwaysVertical _ ->
            bracketClosingRow node

        -- A record update closes with its own `}` when it renders expanded, and
        -- has no closing row of its own when it stays on one line — the same
        -- multi-line-only rule as the bracket lists above, keyed on the same
        -- stored flag `makeRecordUpdateBox` picks its layout from. There was no
        -- arm here at all, so `{ r | a = 1` ⏎ `, b = 2 } {- c -}` fell to the
        -- `-1` default and classified `LeadsOwnLine`: rendered a row below the
        -- declaration's last token, which the reparse re-homes to column 1.
        -- (`prevLineGlueRow` has always had its arm — a `--` is unambiguously
        -- trailing, so it glues after the flat form too.)
        RecordUpdate info ->
            if info.forceVertical then
                bracketClosingRow node

            else
                -1

        -- A when-branch: a same-row comment trails it (`renderWhenBranchesBox`
        -- glues onto the branch's `lpnMaxRow`).
        WhenBranch ->
            (subtreeRowRange node).maxRow

        -- Same reasoning as `prevLineGlueRow`: always multi-line, so a
        -- same-row `{- -}` glues onto the closing `"""`'s row too.
        MultilineString _ ->
            lastRenderedRow node

        -- Unary `-` is a single self-contained unit (its operand IS the
        -- entire content — nothing else can follow inside the `PrefixGlue`,
        -- unlike an `AcrossOrVertical` call flow, which stays open for more
        -- arguments), so — like `UnbreakableText`'s own unconditional
        -- `loc.start.row` and `MultilineString`'s unconditional
        -- `lastRenderedRow` above — a same-row block comment glues
        -- unconditionally, not only when the operand happens to end in a
        -- bracket. (An earlier version of this fix gated on
        -- `bracketClosingRow`, mirroring `AcrossOrVertical`'s call-flow
        -- rule; that left a bracket-free operand's MULTI-LINE trailing
        -- block comment — `-x {- multi⏎line -}` — still oscillating, since
        -- a multi-line comment's placement is decided by `sameRowPrev`
        -- alone, not by the render-layer's separate always-inline-if-
        -- single-line default that masks this gate for single-line
        -- comments.) A lambda head does NOT glue here either way
        -- (`gluesTrailingComment` is `False`) — see the matching case in
        -- `prevLineGlueRow` for why (KitchenComments).
        PrefixGlue loc ->
            if gluesTrailingComment loc.value then
                lastRenderedRow node

            else
                -1

        -- Same fix as `prevLineGlueRow`'s matching arms: glue onto the
        -- previous comment's LAST row, not its first — a multi-line
        -- `{- … -}`'s `-}` closes below its `{-`, and a same-row successor
        -- glues onto that close row. See there for the full rationale.
        SingleLineComment _ ->
            lastRowInSubtree node

        BlockComment _ ->
            lastRowInSubtree node

        _ ->
            -1


-- True for a line/block comment leaf.
isCommentNode : LPNode -> Bool
isCommentNode node =
    when lpnBox node is
        SingleLineComment _ ->
            True

        BlockComment _ ->
            True

        _ ->
            False


{-| Classify a comment being spliced among `before ++ [comment] ++ after`, from
its kind and its immediate neighbours. (The standalone top-level case is handled
by the caller.) A `CommentRole` is a reparse fixed point: the row it is decided
from equals the row it renders on, so a reformat re-derives the same role.

The container decides which glue rule applies — the generic flow is the
*coarse* one (a block comment stays own-line after a call / single-line bracket),
and the "list-like" contexts (binop, bracket lists, union variants, when
branches) are *permissive* (a same-row comment trails any item). Each branch is
pinned by a fixture under `tests/testfiles/` (one directory per suite):

  - binop (`OpAndRhs`/`Binop`) — `BinopChainMixedComments`,
    `MultilineBlockComments` (a comment after a multi-line block comment).
  - bracket list (`AllAcrossOrAllVertical`/`AlwaysVertical`/…) and the
    `RidesInline` flat-ride — `LiteralInlineComment`.
  - union variant (`= Ctor {- c -}`) via `prevBlockGlueRow` — `LiteralInlineComment`.
  - `WhenBranch` predecessor via the glue-rows — `BetweenWhenBranchesComment`.
  - generic flow, elided `->` scoping, and the deliberate `let … in`
    divergence — `LetInTrailingComment`.
-}
classifyCommentKind : LPBox -> LPNode -> Array LPNode -> Array LPNode -> Int -> CommentRole
classifyCommentKind containerBox commentNode before after row =
    let
        -- A binop chain (`Binop` / one `OpAndRhs` step) glues a same-row trailing
        -- comment onto the operand's last line regardless of bracket-ness, and
        -- drops a later-row one to its own line at the operator indent — line and
        -- block comments alike, and relative to the last real OPERAND row plus
        -- any comment run written on from it (`chainedRefRow`). (An older note
        -- here said the rule mirrors `BinopLayout`'s `contentRow`; that function
        -- is gone — a comment-bearing chain renders through the comment-aware
        -- flow, which places each comment from its stored role.)
        isBinopContainer =
            when containerBox is
                OpAndRhs ->
                    True

                Binop _ ->
                    True

                _ ->
                    False

        -- A comment that is a direct child of a bracketed container renders via
        -- `commentBracketListBox`, whose glue rule is *permissive*: a comment on
        -- the same row as the previous item (any item kind — a token, a nested
        -- record, a paren) trails it; an own-row one leads its own line. This
        -- differs from the generic-flow coarse rule, and these comments never
        -- reach the generic `decide` path, so they get their own branch.
        isBracketBranch =
            isBracketContainerBox containerBox

        -- The row a bracket-list child ends on: a bare token by its start (it
        -- cannot span rows), a comment by its LAST row, anything else by its max
        -- row.
        --
        -- A comment is keyed by its last row for the same reason
        -- `prevLineGlueRow` / `prevBlockGlueRow` key one that way in the generic
        -- flow below: a multi-line `{- … -}` closes several rows under its `{-`,
        -- and a comment written after that `-}` sits on the closing row. It
        -- trails the run, exactly as a reader reads it —
        --
        --     , Mango {- mango's
        --         comment -} -- and mango's trailing line comment
        --
        -- — rather than leading the name below. `SortSymbols.takeSameRowTrailing`
        -- chains the same rows, so the whole run travels with `Mango` when the
        -- list sorts.
        bracketItemRow n =
            if isCommentNode n then
                lastRowInSubtree n

            else
                when lpnBox n is
                    UnbreakableText l ->
                        l.start.row

                    _ ->
                        (subtreeRowRange n).maxRow

        bracketPrevRow =
            when prev is
                Just p ->
                    bracketItemRow p

                Nothing ->
                    -1

        -- Last rendered row of the last non-comment sibling before the comment.
        refRow =
            Array.foldl
                (\n acc ->
                    if isCommentNode n then
                        acc

                    else
                        max acc (lastRenderedRow n)
                )
                -1
                before

        -- `refRow` with the trailing comment run chained onto it: a comment
        -- written on the last row of the comment before it joins that run
        -- (`a5d948c`), because a multi-line `{- … -}` closes several rows under
        -- its `{-` and what the author wrote after that `-}` sits beside it, not
        -- on a line of its own. The generic-flow branch keys on the previous
        -- comment's last row (`prevLineGlueRow` / `prevBlockGlueRow`) and the
        -- bracket branch does too (`bracketItemRow`); this is what lets the binop
        -- branch answer the same, instead of dropping the second comment of
        --
        --     a ++ b {- c
        --              second -} {- t -}
        --
        -- to its own row below the chain — where the reparse, seeing a comment
        -- past the declaration's last token, re-homes it to column 1.
        chainedRefRow =
            Array.foldl
                (\n acc ->
                    if isCommentNode n && firstRowInSubtree n <= acc then
                        max acc (lastRowInSubtree n)

                    else
                        acc
                )
                refRow
                before

        prev =
            Array.last before

        next =
            Array.first after

        -- A `--` runs to end-of-line, so it is unambiguously trailing: it glues
        -- onto the previous item's line-glue row (or any elided token), else it
        -- leads its own line. A `--` can never ride inline.
        --
        -- On a top-level declaration line whose trailing tokens are position-less
        -- synthesized text, the immediate `prev` yields no glue row (-1). The one
        -- case is an effect module's `exposing (..)`: its real column depends on
        -- the untracked `where { … }` contents, so `(..)`/`exposing` stay
        -- position-less `SynthesizedText`. Without a glue row a same-row trailing
        -- `-- c` classifies `LeadsOwnLine` and drops to its own line, oscillating
        -- indented↔col-0 on reparse. Fall back to the last real content row on
        -- the line (`refRow`) so it glues onto the header row (`TrailsPrevious`)
        -- instead. (A block trailer already renders inline from `LeadsOwnLine`,
        -- so this only needs to cover the `--` case.)
        --
        -- That tail is position-less over a RANGE of rows, not just one.
        -- `refRow` is the last row the parser recorded anywhere in the header,
        -- and when an effect module's `where { … }` block wraps, the `}` — and
        -- the `exposing (..)` after it — render on the row *below* that, a row
        -- `MakeLogical.moduleLineClosesBelow` puts in the header's
        -- `OriginalRows` range precisely because nothing else records it. A `--`
        -- the author wrote there has no recorded token anywhere on its row, so
        -- `refRow` alone reads it as own-line — and the two formats then
        -- disagree, because the FIRST format's input still had the block's last
        -- field on that row and glued it. Every row from the last recorded
        -- content through the header's last row is the same position-less tail,
        -- so a comment on any of them trails the header. Three conditions, and
        -- each is load-bearing:
        --
        --   - the container is the module line. `info.last` grows past `refRow`
        --     only where a synthesized tail or an elastic close put it there,
        --     which is a module-line fact.
        --   - nothing recorded follows the comment EXCEPT the `exposing` list.
        --     Otherwise a comment the author wrote *before* the `where { … }`
        --     block — on a row of its own, with the whole header still to
        --     come — would read as the tail and glue onto the name above it.
        --     The exposing list is the exception because it is the LAST child
        --     (`[ keyword, name, where…, exposing, exports ]`), so a comment
        --     with only it and the position-less `exposing` keyword after it is
        --     on the tail by construction, whatever rows anything is on. Rows
        --     cannot make that call: both the comment-before-`where` case and
        --     the tail case have recorded content BELOW the comment, so a
        --     row test reads them the same. Without the exception a `{- c -}`
        --     written after a wrapped `where { … }` block's `}` reads as
        --     own-line while the format that produced that row had glued it.
        --   - the row is at or past the last recorded content, and inside the
        --     declaration. Below the header's last row the comment never gets
        --     here: `findOrCreateOrigRow` detaches it to column 1.
        -- The comment sits past an `exposing` keyword that carries NO position —
        -- which `MakeLogical.processModuleLine` emits for an effect module and
        -- only for one: a plain module anchors `exposing` at the end of its
        -- name, an effect module cannot, because the real column depends on the
        -- untracked width of the `where { … }` block.
        --
        -- This is what separates the header's position-less tail from the two
        -- shapes that look identical to it. A `SortSymbols` hoist puts a
        -- sort-to-first comment between `exposing` and `(` with only the list
        -- after it, exactly like a tail comment — but on a PLAIN module, where
        -- the keyword is positioned, so this is False and the hoist keeps its
        -- own line (`ExposingSortCommentToFront`, `SortingCommentZoo`,
        -- `ModuleExposingInlineAndHoistedComment`, `KitchenComments` — all four
        -- broke when the exposing-list exception was added without this).
        pastSynthesizedExposing =
            Array.any
                (\n ->
                    when lpnBox n is
                        SynthesizedText v ->
                            v == "exposing"

                        _ ->
                            False
                )
                before

        --   - the predecessor is not itself a COMMENT. This is a fallback for
        --     the header's position-less *tokens*, and a comment is not one of
        --     them: it carries an exact position, so `prevBlockGlueRow` /
        --     `prevLineGlueRow` already answer honestly for it
        --     (`lastRowInSubtree`, the chaining rule). Firing here anyway
        --     overrode that answer and glued a comment onto the closing row of a
        --     multi-line `{- … -}` it was written *below* — while the same
        --     shape under a plain `module` and an `import` splits and stays
        --     split. A run written on ONE row is unaffected: there the previous
        --     comment's last row IS this comment's row, so the ordinary
        --     `row == glueRow` test glues it without this fallback.
        headerTailGlue p =
            when containerBox is
                OriginalRows info ->
                    info.stype == StModule
                        && not (isCommentNode p)
                        && refRow >= 0
                        && row >= refRow
                        && row <= info.last
                        && Array.all
                            (\n ->
                                lpnFirstPos n == Nothing
                                    || (isExposingListNode n && pastSynthesizedExposing)
                            )
                            after

                _ ->
                    False

        classifyLine p =
            let
                g =
                    prevLineGlueRow p

                glueRow =
                    if g >= 0 then
                        g

                    else
                        when containerBox is
                            OriginalRows _ ->
                                refRow

                            _ ->
                                g
            in
            if nodeIsElided p || headerTailGlue p || (glueRow >= 0 && row == glueRow) then
                TrailsPrevious

            else
                LeadsOwnLine

        -- A single-line `{- -}` on the previous item's row rides the flat line
        -- (`RidesInline` — the only comment kind `literalCommentsRideFlatLine`
        -- keeps flat); a multi-line `{- … -}` on that row glues but breaks the
        -- line (`TrailsPrevious`); an own-row comment leads its own line.
        -- `RidesInline` vs `TrailsPrevious` is observed only by
        -- `literalCommentsRideFlatLine`; every other consumer glues both, so the
        -- distinction need not consider the *next* neighbour.
        --
        -- A position-less `SynthesizedText` predecessor yields no glue row, the
        -- same gap `classifyLine` covers for `--`: an effect module's `exposing`
        -- is synthesized (its real column depends on the untracked `where { … }`
        -- contents), so a `{- c -}` the author wrote on the header row got
        -- `LeadsOwnLine` and rendered on a fresh row below `exposing`. That makes
        -- a FLAT exposing list start below the header, which reparses as a
        -- vertical list — non-idempotent. Fall back to the last real content row
        -- on the line (`refRow`), exactly as the `--` case does.
        --
        -- Scoped to a SINGLE-line comment — the only kind that can actually ride
        -- the header row. A multi-line `{- … -}` breaks the line wherever it
        -- lands, so the fallback would merely move it from its own row onto the
        -- keyword's: `KitchenSink`'s fixture-verified `effect module M where` ⏎
        -- `{- a⏎b⏎c -}` ⏎ `{ command = … }` keeps it own-line.
        --
        -- `headerTailGlue` widens that same fallback from the one row `refRow`
        -- names to the header's whole position-less tail, for the same reason
        -- and under the same single-line scope: a `{- c -}` past a wrapped
        -- `where { … }` block's `}` is on a row nothing records, so `refRow`
        -- alone reads it as own-line while the format that produced that row
        -- had glued it.
        classifyBlock p multiline =
            let
                g =
                    prevBlockGlueRow multiline p

                glueRow =
                    if g >= 0 || multiline then
                        g

                    else
                        when { prev = lpnBox p, container = containerBox } is
                            { prev = SynthesizedText _, container = OriginalRows _ } ->
                                refRow

                            _ ->
                                g

                sameRowPrev =
                    nodeIsElided p
                        || (not multiline && headerTailGlue p)
                        || (glueRow >= 0 && row == glueRow)
            in
            if not sameRowPrev then
                LeadsOwnLine

            else if multiline then
                TrailsPrevious

            else
                RidesInline

        -- Rule **C2** at a bracket list's `,`: the comma carries no source
        -- position, so `1 {- c -} , 2` and `1 , {- c -} 2` arrive as the same
        -- three facts and one of the two must differ from elm-format. A
        -- single-line `{- -}` goes to the item AFTER the separator (`LeadsNext`).
        --
        -- A `--` is C2's one documented exception and stays with the item above
        -- it. A `--` ends its row, so it reads as a note about that row, and here
        -- the row is a complete list item — `[ apple -- the red one` ⏎ `, banana`
        -- is the idiom real code uses (unanimously: every instance in `core/` and
        -- `compiler-common/` is spelled that way, none the other), and it is also
        -- what elm-format produces for that authoring.
        --
        -- Keyed on EITHER adjacent item's row, which is what makes the role a
        -- reparse fixed point. The comment renders on the *next* item's row
        -- (`, {- c -} 2`), so a rule that only recognised "same row as the
        -- previous item" would read its own output as an own-row comment and
        -- drop it to its own line — `[ 1 {- c -}` ⏎ `, 2 ]` oscillated exactly
        -- that way. Both adjacent rows mean the same thing here: the comment is
        -- in the gap, on one side of an invisible comma. Only a comment on
        -- NEITHER row was genuinely written on a line of its own.
        --
        -- Needs a following ITEM, not merely a following sibling: a comment past
        -- the last item has no separator to cross and simply trails it
        -- (`[ 1, 2 {- c -} ]`). The opener slot is excluded by the caller — with
        -- `before` empty the comment already leads the first item.
        --
        -- **Not applied to an exposing list.** `ListParen` is exclusively an
        -- `exposing ( … )` list (see `SortSymbols.gren`), and those are the one
        -- bracket list whose items get REORDERED. `SortSymbols` owns comment
        -- ownership there, on the opposite model — a comment after a name is that
        -- name's and travels with it through the sort, which is what makes
        -- `(a {- c -}, b)` and `(b, a {- c -})` land on the same bytes
        -- (`unfoldLastTrailing`). Flipping the side would have to reshape
        -- clustering and the closing-`)` pinning with it; until that is designed
        -- and put under the `sort-order` oracle, an exposing list keeps the
        -- earlier side.
        -- Also NOT when the item it would lead holds a `"""…"""`. Gluing a
        -- comment onto the front of that item's box shifts its first row by the
        -- comment's width while the string's own content rows move by a
        -- different amount, and Gren requires a multi-line string's rows to be
        -- indented equally — the output stops parsing. The comment stays
        -- own-line there, which is the placement the item's shape forces anyway.
        leadsAcrossItemSeparator =
            not (Array.isEmpty before)
                && not (isExposingListBox containerBox)
                && commentIsSingleLineBlock commentNode
                && gapRunCrossesTogether before after
                && bracketNextItemRow >= 0
                && (row == bracketPrevRow || row == bracketNextItemRow)
                && not nextItemBlocksFrontGlue

        -- The same question inside a record update, whose separator is the
        -- unrecorded `|` and whose "previous item" is the base name — not a
        -- child, so `bracketPrevRow` cannot see it. `RecordUpdate.name.start` is
        -- a real recorded position, and the base is a single token, so its start
        -- row is also its last row.
        updateBaseRow =
            when containerBox is
                RecordUpdate { name } ->
                    name.start.row

                _ ->
                    -1

        leadsAcrossUpdateSeparator =
            commentIsSingleLineBlock commentNode
                && gapRunCrossesTogether before after
                && bracketNextItemRow >= 0
                && (row == updateBaseRow || row == bracketNextItemRow)
                && not nextItemBlocksFrontGlue

        -- The real (non-comment) siblings after the comment; only the first of
        -- them is the one a front-glued comment lands on.
        nextItems =
            Array.keepIf (\n -> not (isCommentNode n)) after

        nextItemBlocksFrontGlue =
            Array.any subtreeHasMultilineString (Array.slice 0 1 nextItems)

        -- First row of the next real ITEM after the comment (-1 when only
        -- comments follow, or nothing does).
        bracketNextItemRow =
            Array.foldl
                (\n acc ->
                    if acc >= 0 then
                        acc

                    else
                        when lpnBox n is
                            UnbreakableText l ->
                                l.start.row

                            _ ->
                                (subtreeRowRange n).minRow
                )
                -1
                nextItems

        -- The comment is somewhere in the container's OPENER RUN: the leading
        -- comments, with no item yet before them (`[ {- a -} {- b -} 1`). Rule
        -- **C1** says one gap is one attachment, so the whole run is one thing
        -- and gets one role -- `before` being all comments, rather than empty, is
        -- what says the second member is still in the same slot as the first.
        --
        -- The record update's arm has read it that way since `b544d53` (its own
        -- comment: "`before` being all comments (rather than empty) is what keeps
        -- a SECOND comment here in the same region as the first"); the item list
        -- asked `Array.isEmpty before` and so split its runs, giving the first
        -- member `RidesInline` and every later one `LeadsNext` or `LeadsOwnLine`.
        -- Look for the container that already agrees.
        --
        -- Nothing rendered differently, because at this slot the renderer does
        -- not read the role: `commentBracketListBox` takes `atOpener` from its
        -- own fold and the comment's SHAPE from `commentTextCanRide`. That is
        -- exactly how the split drifted in unnoticed -- a stored fact nobody
        -- consults is a stored fact free to be wrong -- and it is why
        -- `commentRidesInline`'s docstring has to warn that a shape-ridable
        -- comment may not be role-ridable. It no longer can be.
        inOpenerRun =
            Array.all isCommentNode before

        -- A comment that glues to whatever precedes it inside a bracket, by its
        -- own shape: a single-line `{- -}` rides the line (`RidesInline`, the
        -- only kind `literalCommentsRideFlatLine` keeps flat); a `--` or a
        -- multi-line `{- … -}` glues but breaks it.
        bracketKindRole =
            when lpnBox commentNode is
                BlockComment { loc } ->
                    if String.contains "\n" loc.value then
                        TrailsPrevious

                    else
                        RidesInline

                _ ->
                    TrailsPrevious
    in
    if isBracketBranch then
        -- A `RecordUpdate`'s children are its FIELDS, so where a comment sits
        -- relative to them says nothing. What decides it is the base name, whose
        -- start position IS recorded (`RecordUpdate.name.start`):
        --
        --   before the base → the OPENER slot, `{ {- c -} rec | a = 1 }`, known
        --       to be inside the braces because the `{` is recorded too. Glues to
        --       the `{` exactly as an item list's first comment does.
        --   after the base  → the ambiguous around-the-`|` slot, decided exactly
        --       as a bracket list's `,` gap is, with the base standing in for the
        --       item above. Rule **C2** for a single-line `{- -}` (`LeadsNext` →
        --       `| {- c -} a = 1`, both authorings alike, since the `|` carries no
        --       position); for a `--` or a multi-line `{- … -}`, the row it was
        --       written on, because a `|` LEADS its line and a comment above it
        --       therefore strands nothing:
        --
        --           { rec -- c            the base's row  → `TrailsHead`
        --               | a = 1 }
        --
        --           { rec                 its own row     → `LeadsOwnLine`,
        --               -- c                                which the renderer
        --               | a = 1 }                           puts at the `|` column
        --
        --       This is what `,` and a union's `|` already do; the record update
        --       used to send both spellings to the field after the separator, the
        --       one line-leading separator that did.
        --
        -- Everywhere the renderer needs to tell the two apart it can: the opener
        -- slot is the leading run of comment children whose role is none of
        -- `LeadsNext` / `LeadsOwnLine` / `TrailsHead`. A comment TRAILING a field
        -- is not in that leading run and keeps the ordinary same-row rules.
        if isRecordUpdateBox containerBox && Array.all isCommentNode before then
            -- Somewhere in the base … first-field region. `before` being all
            -- comments (rather than empty) is what keeps a SECOND comment here
            -- in the same region as the first — `{ {- a -} rec {- b -} | x = 1 }`
            -- must not let `{- b -}` inherit the opener's same-row glue.
            if commentPrecedesUpdateBase containerBox commentNode then
                bracketKindRole

            else if leadsAcrossUpdateSeparator then
                LeadsNext

            else if updateBaseRow >= 0 && row /= updateBaseRow then
                LeadsOwnLine

            else
                TrailsHead

        else if
            -- Nothing before it inside an ITEM LIST → it trails the OPENING bracket
            -- (`[ {- c -} 1, 2 ]`). The descent only lets a comment land here when it
            -- was written past the recorded `{`/`[`, so "first child" IS "written
            -- just after the opener"; it glues to the opener exactly as a same-row
            -- comment glues to the item before it, and for the same reason a
            -- single-line `{- -}` may still ride the flat line.
            --
            -- Otherwise: own-row → leads its own line at the bracket column. Same-row
            -- as the previous item → glues: a single-line `{- -}` rides the flat line
            -- (`RidesInline`, the only kind `literalCommentsRideFlatLine` keeps
            -- flat); a `--` or a multi-line `{- … -}` glues but breaks the line
            -- (`TrailsPrevious`).
            leadsAcrossItemSeparator && not inOpenerRun
        then
            LeadsNext

        else if not (Array.isEmpty before) && not inOpenerRun && (bracketPrevRow < 0 || row /= bracketPrevRow) then
            LeadsOwnLine

        else if inOpenerRun && nextItemBlocksFrontGlue then
            -- The opener slot (`[ {- c -} item`), where the first item holds a
            -- `"""…"""`. Same reason a `LeadsNext` comment gives way there: the
            -- front glue shifts the item's first row by the comment's width
            -- while the string's own rows move by a different amount, and Gren
            -- requires them indented equally, so the output stops parsing. (A
            -- comment gluing onto the item BEFORE it is a suffix, landing after
            -- the closing `"""`, and is fine — this is only about the front.)
            LeadsOwnLine

        else
            bracketKindRole

    else if isBinopContainer then
        -- Same-row-as-operand trails inline; a later-row comment leads its own
        -- line at the operator indent (relative to `chainedRefRow` — the last
        -- operand, plus any trailing comment run written on from its row).
        if row > chainedRefRow then
            LeadsOwnLine

        else
            TrailsPrevious

    else
        when prev is
            Nothing ->
                -- Nothing precedes: the comment leads the flow.
                LeadsOwnLine

            Just p ->
                when lpnBox commentNode is
                    SingleLineComment _ ->
                        classifyLine p

                    BlockComment { loc } ->
                        classifyBlock p (String.contains "\n" loc.value)

                    _ ->
                        LeadsOwnLine
